', NULL, N'Administrative-2.1.1', N'2.1.1', N'Security Policy and Procedures', N'Security Policy/Proced', N'The organization develops, implements, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, control system security policy that addresses:
a. The purpose of the security program as it relates to protecting the organization''s personnel and assets.
b. The scope of the security program as it applies to all organizational staff and third-party contractors.
c. The roles, responsibilities, management commitment, and coordination among organizational entities of the security program to ensure compliance with the organization''s security policy and other regulatory commitments.
2. Formal, documented procedures to implement the security policy and associated requirements. A control system security policy considers controls from each family contained in this document.', N'The security policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The control system security policy can be included as part of the general security policy for the organization. Procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for the control system in particular, when required.

', NULL, N'The organization develops, implements, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, control system security policy that addresses:
    2. The purpose of the security program as it relates to protecting the organization’s personnel and assets
    2. The scope of the security program as it applies to all organizational staff and third-party contractors
    2. The roles, responsibilities, management commitment, and coordination among organizational entities of the security program to ensure compliance with the organization’s security policy and other regulatory commitments.
2. Formal, documented procedures to implement the security policy and associated requirements. A control system security policy considers controls from each family contained in this document.
', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Security Policy and Procedures</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security policies are an extension of higher-level organizational policies with consideration for identified risks. Procedures implement the policies and allow the organization to communicate to employees, third party contractors, and vendors how the security program will be managed.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops, implements, and periodically reviews and updates:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">A formal, documented, control system security policy that addresses:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The purpose of the security program as it relates to protecting the organization’s personnel and assets</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The scope of the security program as it applies to all organizational staff and third-party contractors</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The roles, responsibilities, management commitment, and coordination among organizational entities of the security program to ensure compliance with the organization’s security policy and other regulatory commitments.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Formal, documented procedures to implement the security policy and associated requirements. A control system security policy considers controls from each family contained in this document.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The security policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The control system security policy can be included as part of the general security policy for the organization. Procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for the control system in particular, when required.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 AC-1, SC-14, PM-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-9</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 4, 5.5, 7.1.3, Annex A, Annex B.4.1.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3, B.R1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. A.2, App. B.1.1</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.2.1', N'2.2.1', N'Management Policy and Procedures', N'Mgmt. Policy/Proced', N'The organization establishes policies and procedures to define roles, responsibilities, behaviors, and practices for the implementation of an overall security program.', N'The scope and responsibilities of the security program include management accountability, physical security, and information security for the enterprise and control systems. This program applies to thirdparty contractors, outsourcing partners, and the supply chain components of the organization.

', NULL, N'The organization establishes policies and procedures to define roles, responsibilities, behaviors, and practices for the implementation of an overall security program.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Management Policy and Procedures</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Organizational security involves setting organization wide policies and procedures that define acceptable employee behavior and practices concerning security. Management policy and procedures document management''s roles, responsibilities, and authorities within the organizational security program.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization establishes policies and procedures to define roles, responsibilities, behaviors, and practices for the implementation of an overall security program.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The scope and responsibilities of the security program include management accountability, physical security, and information security for the enterprise and control systems. This program applies to thirdparty contractors, outsourcing partners, and the supply chain components of the organization.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PM-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 1.2, Annex A, Annex B.4.1.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 002-3 through CIP 009-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.11</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.2.2', N'2.2.2', N'Management Accountability', N'Mgmt. Account', N'The organization defines a framework of management leadership accountability. This framework establishes roles and responsibilities to approve cybersecurity policy, assign security roles, and coordinate the implementation of cybersecurity across the organization.', N'This framework is not limited to traditional IT systems but also extends to control systems and the organization’s supply chain.

', NULL, N'The organization defines a framework of management leadership accountability. This framework establishes roles and responsibilities to approve cybersecurity policy, assign security roles, and coordinate the implementation of cybersecurity across the organization.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Management Accountability</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security policies document the control and behavior expectations that each member of the organization''s staff is required to meet in the daily operation of the control system. Management accountability is the high level organizational framework for managing the security program.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization defines a framework of management leadership accountability. This framework establishes roles and responsibilities to approve cybersecurity policy, assign security roles, and coordinate the implementation of cybersecurity across the organization.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">This framework is not limited to traditional IT systems but also extends to control systems and the organization’s supply chain.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PM-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 1.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3, B.R2, B.R5</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.2.3', N'2.2.3', N'Baseline Practices', N'Baseline Practices', N'Baseline practices that organizations employ for organizational security include, but are not limited to:

a) Executive management accountability for the security program.
b) Responsibility for control system security within the organization includes sufficient authority and an appropriate level of funding to implement the organization''s security policy.
c) The organization''s security policies and procedures that provide clear direction, accountability, and oversight for the organization''s security team. The security team assigns roles and responsibilities in accordance with the organization''s policies and confirms that processes are in place to protect company assets and critical information.
d) The organization''s contracts with external entities that address the organization''s security policies and procedures with business partners, third-party contractors, and outsourcing partners.
e) The organization''s security policies and procedures that ensure coordination or integration with the organization''s physical security plan. Organization roles and responsibilities are established that address the overlap and synergy between physical and control system security risks.', N'None

', NULL, N'Baseline practices that organizations employ for organizational security include, but are not limited to:

1. Executive management accountability for the security program.
2. Responsibility for control system security within the organization includes sufficient authority and an appropriate level of funding to implement the organization’s security policy.
3. The organization’s security policies and procedures that provide clear direction, accountability, and oversight for the organization’s security team. The security team assigns roles and responsibilities in accordance with the organization’s policies and confirms that processes are in place to protect company assets and critical information.
4. The organization’s contracts with external entities that address the organization’s security policies and procedures with business partners, third-party contractors, and outsourcing partners.
5. The organization’s security policies and procedures that ensure coordination or integration with the organization’s physical security plan. Organization roles and responsibilities are established that address the overlap and synergy between physical and control system security risks.
', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Baseline Practices</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security policies document the control and behavior expectations that each member of the organization''s staff is required to meet in the daily operation of the control system. Baseline practices are those fundamental activities employed by organizations as part of a sound organizational security program.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Baseline practices that organizations employ for organizational security include, but are not limited to:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Executive management accountability for the security program.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Responsibility for control system security within the organization includes sufficient authority and an appropriate level of funding to implement the organization’s security policy.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization’s security policies and procedures that provide clear direction, accountability, and oversight for the organization’s security team. The security team assigns roles and responsibilities in accordance with the organization’s policies and confirms that processes are in place to protect company assets and critical information.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization’s contracts with external entities that address the organization’s security policies and procedures with business partners, third-party contractors, and outsourcing partners.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization’s security policies and procedures that ensure coordination or integration with the organization’s physical security plan. Organization roles and responsibilities are established that address the overlap and synergy between physical and control system security risks.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PM-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5, 3.6, 3.7, 3.8</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.11.3</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.2.4', N'2.2.4', N'Coordination of Threat Mitigation', N'Threat Mitigation', N'The organization''s security policies and procedures delineate how the organization implements its emergency response plan and coordinates efforts with law enforcement agencies, regulators, Internet service providers, and other relevant organizations in the event of a security incident.', N'The organization expands relationships with local emergency response personnel to include information sharing and coordination of contingency plans as well as coordinated response to cybersecurity incidents. Entities, such as US-CERT and ICS-CERT, are available for threat assistance.

', NULL, N'The organization’s security policies and procedures delineate how the organization implements its emergency response plan and coordinates efforts with law enforcement agencies, regulators, Internet service providers, and other relevant organizations in the event of a security incident.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Coordination of Threat Mitigation</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security policies document the control and behavior expectations that each member of the organization''s staff is required to meet in the daily operation of the control system. Coordination of threat mitigation is that aspect of the organizational security policy that explains emergency response coordination with third parties.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization’s security policies and procedures delineate how the organization implements its emergency response plan and coordinates efforts with law enforcement agencies, regulators, Internet service providers, and other relevant organizations in the event of a security incident.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization expands relationships with local emergency response personnel to include information sharing and coordination of contingency plans as well as coordinated response to cybersecurity incidents. Entities, such as US-CERT and ICS-CERT, are available for threat assistance.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PM-9</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Section 3, Annex B and B.3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3, B.R1</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.2.5', N'2.2.5', N'Security Policies for Third Parties', N'Third-Party Policy', N'The organization holds external suppliers and contractors that have an impact on the security of the control center to the same security policies and procedures as the organization''s own personnel. The organization ensures security policies and procedures of second and third-tier suppliers comply with corporate cybersecurity policies and procedures if they will impact control system security.', N'The organization considers the increased security risk associated with outsourcing as part of the decision-making process to determine what to outsource and what outsourcing partner to select. Contracts with external suppliers govern physical as well as logical access. The organization clearly defines confidentiality or nondisclosure agreements and intellectual property rights. The organization also clearly defines change management procedures.

', NULL, N'The organization holds external suppliers and contractors that have an impact on the security of the control center to the same security policies and procedures as the organization’s own personnel. The organization ensures security policies and procedures of second and third-tier suppliers comply with corporate cybersecurity policies and procedures if they will impact control system security.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Security Policies for Third Parties</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security policies document the control and behavior expectations that each member of the organization''s staff is required to meet in the daily operation of the control system. Security policies for third parties is that aspect of the organizational security policy that explains security expectations for external suppliers and contractors.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization holds external suppliers and contractors that have an impact on the security of the control center to the same security policies and procedures as the organization’s own personnel. The organization ensures security policies and procedures of second and third-tier suppliers comply with corporate cybersecurity policies and procedures if they will impact control system security.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization considers the increased security risk associated with outsourcing as part of the decision-making process to determine what to outsource and what outsourcing partner to select. Contracts with external suppliers govern physical as well as logical access. The organization clearly defines confidentiality or nondisclosure agreements and intellectual property rights. The organization also clearly defines change management procedures.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PS-7</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.4, 7.3.4, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 004-3, B.R4, B.R4.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.21</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.2.6', N'2.2.6', N'Termination of Third-Party Access', N'Third-Party Access', N'The organization establishes procedures to remove external supplier physical and electronic access at the conclusion/termination of the contract in a timely manner.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization periodically reviews existing authorized physical and electronic access permissions to ensure they are current. This check provides validation that terminated entities have been removed from physical and electronic access.', N'The organization clearly defines the timeliness for removal of external supplier access in the contract.

', NULL, N'The organization establishes procedures to remove external supplier physical and electronic access at the conclusion/termination of the contract in a timely manner.

', NULL, N'1. The organization periodically reviews existing authorized physical and electronic access permissions to ensure they are current. This check provides validation that terminated entities have been removed from physical and electronic access.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Termination of Third-Party Access</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security policies document the control and behavior expectations that each member of the organization''s staff is required to meet in the daily operation of the control system. Termination of third party access is that aspect of the organizational security policy that defines how and when physical and electronic access permissions for external suppliers and contractors will be discontinued.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization establishes procedures to remove external supplier physical and electronic access at the conclusion/termination of the contract in a timely manner.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization clearly defines the timeliness for removal of external supplier access in the contract.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization periodically reviews existing authorized physical and electronic access permissions to ensure they are current. This check provides validation that terminated entities have been removed from physical and electronic access.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 AC-2, PS-4</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-9, CC-11</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 7.3.4, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 004-3, B.R4, B.4.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.21, App. C.2.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.3.1', N'2.3.1', N'Personnel Security Policy and Procedures', N'Personnel Policy/Proced', N'The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, personnel security policy that addresses:
a. The purpose of the security program as it relates to protecting the organization''s personnel and assets.
b. The scope of the security program as it applies to all the organizational staff and third-party contractors.
c. The roles, responsibilities, management commitment, and coordination among organizational entities of the security program to ensure compliance with the organization''s security policy and other regulatory commitments.
2. Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the personnel security policy and associated personnel security controls
3. Formal procedures to review and document the list of approved personnel with access to control systems.', N'The organization ensures the personnel security policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The personnel security policy can be included as part of the general information security policy for the organization. Personnel security procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for a particular control system, when required.

', NULL, N'The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, personnel security policy that addresses:
    2. The purpose of the security program as it relates to protecting the organization’s personnel and assets
    2. The scope of the security program as it applies to all the organizational staff and third-party contractors
    2. The roles, responsibilities, management commitment, and coordination among organizational entities of the security program to ensure compliance with the organization’s security policy and other regulatory commitments
2. Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the personnel security policy and associated personnel security controls
3. Formal procedures to review and document the list of approved personnel with access to control systems.
', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Personnel Security Policy and Procedures</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Personnel security policies are an extension of higher-level organizational security policies. Procedures implement the policies and allow the organization to communicate their security-related requirements to employees, third party contractors, and vendors.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">A formal, documented, personnel security policy that addresses:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The purpose of the security program as it relates to protecting the organization’s personnel and assets</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The scope of the security program as it applies to all the organizational staff and third-party contractors</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The roles, responsibilities, management commitment, and coordination among organizational entities of the security program to ensure compliance with the organization’s security policy and other regulatory commitments</Paragraph></ListItem></List></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the personnel security policy and associated personnel security controls</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Formal procedures to review and document the list of approved personnel with access to control systems.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization ensures the personnel security policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The personnel security policy can be included as part of the general information security policy for the organization. Personnel security procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for a particular control system, when required.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PS-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3, A, B.R1, B.1.1-1.3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.21, App. C.2.1</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.3.2', N'2.3.2', N'Position Categorization', N'Position Categorization', N'The organization assigns a risk designation to all positions and establishes screening criteria for individuals filling those positions. The organization reviews and revises position risk designations periodically based on the organization''s requirements or regulatory commitments.', N'Designated officials within the organization assign a risk level for every position within the control system as determined by the position''s potential for adverse impact to the integrity and efficiency of the control system.

', NULL, N'The organization assigns a risk designation to all positions and establishes screening criteria for individuals filling those positions. The organization reviews and revises position risk designations periodically based on the organization’s requirements or regulatory commitments.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Position Categorization</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Personnel security procedures document the specific security expectations that each member of the organization''s staff is required to meet. Position categorization is an aspect of personnel security that assigns risk to all staff positions related to control systems.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization assigns a risk designation to all positions and establishes screening criteria for individuals filling those positions. The organization reviews and revises position risk designations periodically based on the organization’s requirements or regulatory commitments.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Designated officials within the organization assign a risk level for every position within the control system as determined by the position''s potential for adverse impact to the integrity and efficiency of the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PS-2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3, B.R5.1, B.5.1.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.21</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.3.3', N'2.3.3', N'Personnel Screening', N'Personnel Screening', N'The organization screens individuals requiring access to the control system before access is authorized.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization rescreens individuals with access to organizational control systems based on a defined list of conditions requiring rescreening and the frequency of such rescreening.', N'The organization maintains consistency between the screening process and organizational policy, regulations, guidance, and the criteria established for the risk designation of the assigned position.

Basic screening requirements include:

1. Past 5 years of employment
2. Past 5 years of education, with verification of the highest degree received
3. Past 3 years of residency
4. References
5. Past 5 years of law enforcement records.
', NULL, N'The organization screens individuals requiring access to the control system before access is authorized.

', NULL, N'1. The organization rescreens individuals with access to organizational control systems based on a defined list of conditions requiring rescreening and the frequency of such rescreening.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Personnel Screening</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Personnel security procedures document the specific security expectations that each member of the organization''s staff is required to meet. Personnel screening is that aspect of personnel security that reviews the background of potential employees.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization screens individuals requiring access to the control system before access is authorized.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization maintains consistency between the screening process and organizational policy, regulations, guidance, and the criteria established for the risk designation of the assigned position.</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Basic screening requirements include:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Past 5 years of employment</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Past 5 years of education, with verification of the highest degree received</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Past 3 years of residency</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">References</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Past 5 years of law enforcement records.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization rescreens individuals with access to organizational control systems based on a defined list of conditions requiring rescreening and the frequency of such rescreening.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PS-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 004-3, B.R5.1, B.R5.1.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.21</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.3.4', N'2.3.4', N'Personnel Termination', N'Personnel Termination', N'When an employee is terminated, the organization revokes logical and physical access to control systems and facilities and ensures all organization-owned property is returned and that organization-owned documents and data files relating to the control system that are in the employee''s possession are transferred to the new authorized owner within the organization. Complete execution of this control occurs within 24 hours for employees or contractors terminated for cause.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization implements automated processes to revoke access permissions that are initiated by the termination. Periodic reviews of physical and electronic access are conducted to validate terminated account access are removed.', N'Organization-owned property includes system administration manuals, keys, identification cards, building passes, computers, cell phones, and personal data assistants. Organization-owned documents include field device configuration and operational information, control system network documentation. Exit interviews ensure that individuals understand any security constraints imposed by being a former employee and that proper accountability is achieved for all system-related property.

', NULL, N'When an employee is terminated, the organization revokes logical and physical access to control systems and facilities and ensures all organization-owned property is returned and that organizationowned documents and data files relating to the control system that are in the employee’s possession are transferred to the new authorized owner within the organization. Complete execution of this control occurs within 24 hours for employees or contractors terminated for cause.

', NULL, N'1. The organization implements automated processes to revoke access permissions that are initiated by the termination. Periodic reviews of physical and electronic access are conducted to validate terminated account access are removed.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Personnel Termination</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Personnel security procedures document the specific security expectations that each member of the organization''s staff is required to meet. Personnel termination is that aspect of personnel security that defines how and when a terminated employee''s access to control systems will be cancelled.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">When an employee is terminated, the organization revokes logical and physical access to control systems and facilities and ensures all organization-owned property is returned and that organizationowned documents and data files relating to the control system that are in the employee’s possession are transferred to the new authorized owner within the organization. Complete execution of this control occurs within 24 hours for employees or contractors terminated for cause.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Organization-owned property includes system administration manuals, keys, identification cards, building passes, computers, cell phones, and personal data assistants. Organization-owned documents include field device configuration and operational information, control system network documentation. Exit interviews ensure that individuals understand any security constraints imposed by being a former employee and that proper accountability is achieved for all system-related property.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization implements automated processes to revoke access permissions that are initiated by the termination. Periodic reviews of physical and electronic access are conducted to validate terminated account access are removed.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PS-4</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 004-3, B.R4, B.4.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.2, App. C.2.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.3.5', N'2.3.5', N'Personnel Transfer', N'Personnel Transfer', N'The organization reviews electronic and physical access permissions to control systems and facilities when individuals are reassigned or transferred to other positions within the organization and initiates appropriate actions. Complete execution of this control occurs within 7 days for employees or contractors who no longer need to access control system resources.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization periodically reviews existing authorized physical and electronic access permissions to ensure they are current. This check is to provide validation that transferred entities have been added, changed, or removed correctly from necessary physical and electronic access.', N'Appropriate actions may include:

1. Returning old and issuing new keys, identification cards, and building passes
2. Closing old accounts and establishing new accounts
3. Changing system access authorizations
4. Providing access to official records created or managed by the employee at the former work location and in the former accounts.
