 that the organization deems to be of significant risk in accordance with organizational policies and procedures.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Mobile devices include portable storage media (e.g., USB [Universal Serial Bus] memory sticks, external hard disk drives) and portable computing and communications devices with storage capability (e.g., notebook computers, personal digital assistants, cellular telephones). Usage restrictions and implementation guidance related to mobile devices can include configuration management, device identification and authentication, implementation of mandatory protective software (e.g., malicious code detection, firewall), scanning devices for malicious code, updating virus protection software, scanning for critical software updates and patches, conducting primary operating system (and possibly other resident software) integrity checks, and disabling unnecessary hardware (e.g., wireless, infrared). Examples of control system functionality that provide the capability for automatic execution of code are AutoRun and AutoPlay.</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Organizational policies and procedures for mobile devices used by individuals departing on and returning from travel include determining which locations are of concern, defining required configurations for the devices, ensuring that the devices are configured as intended before travel is initiated, and applying specific measures to the device after travel is completed. Specially configured mobile devices include computers with sanitized hard drives, limited applications, and additional hardening (e.g., more stringent configuration settings). Specified measures applied to mobile devices upon return from travel include examining the device for signs of physical tampering and purging/reimaging the hard disk drive. Protecting information residing on mobile devices is covered in the media protection family.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization restricts the use of writable, removable media in organizational control systems.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization prohibits the use of personally owned, removable media in organizational control systems.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization prohibits the use of removable media in organizational control systems when the media have no identifiable owner.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 MP-2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 007-3, B.R7.1, R7.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-15</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.4.17', N'2.4.17', N'Personnel and Asset Tracking', N'Personnel/Asset Tracking', N'The organization implements asset location technologies to track and monitor the movements of personnel and vehicles within the organization''s controlled areas to ensure they stay in authorized areas, to identify personnel needing assistance, and to support emergency response.
Requirement Enhancement - Electronic monitoring mechanisms alert control system personnel when unauthorized access or an emergency occurs.', N'None

', NULL, N'The organization implements asset location technologies to track and monitor the movements of personnel and vehicles within the organization’s controlled areas to ensure they stay in authorized areas, to identify personnel needing assistance, and to support emergency response.

', NULL, N'1. Electronic monitoring mechanisms alert control system personnel when unauthorized access or an emergency occurs.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Personnel and Asset Tracking</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Physical and environmental security encompasses protection of physical assets from damage, misuse, or theft. Personnel and asset tracking is an aspect of physical and environmental security. It deals with monitoring personnel and vehicles when within a secure area.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization implements asset location technologies to track and monitor the movements of personnel and vehicles within the organization’s controlled areas to ensure they stay in authorized areas, to identify personnel needing assistance, and to support emergency response.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Electronic monitoring mechanisms alert control system personnel when unauthorized access or an emergency occurs.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.4.18', N'2.4.18', N'Location of Control System Assets', N'Sys Asset Location', N'The organization locates control system assets to minimize potential damage from physical and environmental hazards and to minimize the opportunity for unauthorized access.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization considers the risks associated with physical and environmental hazards when planning new control system facilities or reviewing existing facilities. Risk mitigation strategies are documented in the control system security plan.', N'Physical and environmental hazards include flooding, fire, tornados, earthquakes, hurricanes, acts of terrorism, vandalism, electrical interference, and electromagnetic radiation. Where a completely enclosed (sixwall) border cannot be established, the organization implements and documents alternate measures to control physical access to the control system assets. Whenever possible, the organization also considers the location or site of the facility with regard to physical and environmental hazards. This control may be satisfied by similar requirements fulfilled by another organizational entity other than the control system security program. Organizations should avoid duplicating actions already covered.

', NULL, N'The organization locates control system assets to minimize potential damage from physical and environmental hazards and to minimize the opportunity for unauthorized access.

', NULL, N'1. The organization considers the risks associated with physical and environmental hazards when planning new control system facilities or reviewing existing facilities. Risk mitigation strategies are documented in the control system security plan.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Location of Control System Assets</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Physical and environmental security encompasses protection of physical assets from damage, misuse, or theft. Location of control system assets is an aspect of physical and environmental security. It deals with placing control systems away from potential damage from physical and environmental hazards.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization locates control system assets to minimize potential damage from physical and environmental hazards and to minimize the opportunity for unauthorized access.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Physical and environmental hazards include flooding, fire, tornados, earthquakes, hurricanes, acts of terrorism, vandalism, electrical interference, and electromagnetic radiation. Where a completely enclosed (sixwall) border cannot be established, the organization implements and documents alternate measures to control physical access to the control system assets. Whenever possible, the organization also considers the location or site of the facility with regard to physical and environmental hazards. This control may be satisfied by similar requirements fulfilled by another organizational entity other than the control system security program. Organizations should avoid duplicating actions already covered.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization considers the risks associated with physical and environmental hazards when planning new control system facilities or reviewing existing facilities. Risk mitigation strategies are documented in the control system security plan.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PE-18</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 002-3, B.R1-R4, CIP 006-3, B.R1.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.2, C.3.1, C.3.1.3, C.3.3.3, C.3.3.2.9, C.3.14, C.4</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.4.19', N'2.4.19', N'Information Leakage', N'Info Leakage', N'The organization protects the control system from information leakage.', N'The organization considers all forms of information leakage such as removable media, official documents, remote access, misconfigured perimeter security devices, and electromagnetic signals emanations. This requirement supports confidentiality more than availability and, hence, is not as critical for control system applications.

The FIPS 199 security categorization (for confidentiality) of the system and organizational security policy guides the application of safeguards and countermeasures employed to protect the system against information leakage because of electromagnetic signals emanations.

', NULL, N'The organization protects the control system from information leakage.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information Leakage</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Physical and environmental security encompasses protection of physical assets from damage, misuse, or theft. Information leakage is an aspect of physical and environmental security. It deals with preventing the loss of information through physical, electronic, or electromagnetic means.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization protects the control system from information leakage.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization considers all forms of information leakage such as removable media, official documents, remote access, misconfigured perimeter security devices, and electromagnetic signals emanations. This requirement supports confidentiality more than availability and, hence, is not as critical for control system applications.</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The FIPS 199 security categorization (for confidentiality) of the system and organizational security policy guides the application of safeguards and countermeasures employed to protect the system against information leakage because of electromagnetic signals emanations.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PE-19</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.3.1.3 , C.3.2, App. B.2.5, App. B.5.1, App. C.2.1, App. C.3.4, App. C.3.11, App. C.6, App. C.8, App. C.9</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.4.20', N'2.4.20', N'Power Equipment and Power Cabling', N'Power Equip/Cabling', N'The organization protects control system power equipment and power cabling from damage and destruction.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization employs redundant power equipment and parallel power cabling paths for the control system.', N'None

', NULL, N'The organization protects control system power equipment and power cabling from damage and destruction.

', NULL, N'1. The organization employs redundant power equipment and parallel power cabling paths for the control system.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Power Equipment and Power Cabling</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Physical and environmental security encompasses protection of physical assets from damage, misuse, or theft. Power equipment and power cabling is an aspect of physical and environmental security. It deals with the protection of power equipment and power cables.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization protects control system power equipment and power cabling from damage and destruction.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs redundant power equipment and parallel power cabling paths for the control system.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PE-9</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 4, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.2, C.3.1, C.3.3.2.9</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.4.21', N'2.4.21', N'Physical Device Access Control', N'Device Access Cntrl', N'The organization employs hardware (cages, locks, cases, etc.) to detect and deter unauthorized physical access to control system devices.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization ensures that the ability to respond appropriately in the event of an emergency is not hindered by using tamper-evident hardware.', N'Tamper-evident hardware includes, but is not limited to: (1) metal or hard plastic production-grade enclosures, (2) opaque enclosures with tamper-evident seals or pick-resistant locks for doors or removable covers, and (3) tamper detection/response envelopes with tamper response.

', NULL, N'The organization employs hardware (cages, locks, cases, etc.) to detect and deter unauthorized physical access to control system devices.

', NULL, N'1. The organization ensures that the ability to respond appropriately in the event of an emergency is not hindered by using tamper-evident hardware.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Physical Device Access Control</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Physical and environmental security encompasses protection of physical assets from damage, misuse, or theft. Physical device access control is an aspect of physical and environmental security. It deals with the use of physical devices and structures to prevent physical access to the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization employs hardware (cages, locks, cases, etc.) to detect and deter unauthorized physical access to control system devices.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Tamper-evident hardware includes, but is not limited to: (1) metal or hard plastic production-grade enclosures, (2) opaque enclosures with tamper-evident seals or pick-resistant locks for doors or removable covers, and (3) tamper detection/response envelopes with tamper response.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization ensures that the ability to respond appropriately in the event of an emergency is not hindered by using tamper-evident hardware.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 PE-3, PE-4, PE-5</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 5.9, 8.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.3.3.2.5, App. B.1.1, App. B.1.11, App. B.1.15, App. B.1.19, App. B.4.2, App. B.4.5, App. C.3.1, App. C.5.5, App. C.5.6</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.5.1', N'2.5.1', N'System and Services Acquisition Policy and Procedures', N'Acq Policy/Proced', N'The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, system and services acquisition policy that includes control system security considerations and that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance.
2. Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the system and services acquisition policy and associated system and services acquisition controls.', N'The organization ensures the system and services acquisition policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The system and services acquisition policy can be included as part of the general information security policy for the organization. System and services acquisition procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for a particular control system when required.

', NULL, N'The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, system and services acquisition policy that includes control system security considerations and that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance
2. Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the system and services acquisition policy and associated system and services acquisition controls.
', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">System and Services Acquisition Policy and Procedures</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and services acquisition policies are an extension of higher-level organizational security policies. Procedures implement the policies and allow the organization to communicate their security-related requirements to employees, third party contractors, and vendors.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">A formal, documented, system and services acquisition policy that includes control system security considerations and that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the system and services acquisition policy and associated system and services acquisition controls.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization ensures the system and services acquisition policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The system and services acquisition policy can be included as part of the general information security policy for the organization. System and services acquisition procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for a particular control system when required.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SA-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.3.4, C.3.3.3, C.3.3.3.1</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.5.2', N'2.5.2', N'Allocation of Resources', N'Res Allocation', N'The organization:

