/ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system routes all internal communications traffic to the Internet through authenticated proxy servers within the managed interfaces of boundary protection devices.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization selects an appropriate failure mode (e.g., fail open or fail close), depending on the critical needs of system availability.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-7</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-4, CC-5, CC-13, CC-15, CC-16</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 4, Annex A, Annex B</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 005-3. A, B.R1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.3.2.1, App. B.1.20</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.8', N'2.8.8', N'Communication Integrity', N'Comm Integrity', N'The control system design and implementation protects the integrity of electronically communicated information.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to ensure recognition of changes to information during transmission unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures (e.g., protective distribution systems).
Requirement Enhancement 2 - The use of cryptography within a control system will introduce latency to control system communication. The latency introduced from the use of cryptographic mechanisms must not degrade the operational performance of the control system or impact personnel safety.
Requirement Enhancement 3 - Failure of a cryptographic mechanism must not create a denial of service. Control systems generally support the objectives of availability, integrity, and confidentiality. Therefore, the use of cryptography should be determined after careful consideration.
Requirement Enhancement 4 - The control system maintains the integrity of information during aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.', N'If the organization is relying on a commercial service provider for communication services as a commodity item rather than a fully dedicated service, it may be more difficult to obtain the necessary assurances regarding the implementation of needed security measures for transmission integrity. When it is infeasible or impractical to obtain the necessary assurances of effective security through appropriate contracting vehicles, the organization either implements appropriate compensating security measures or explicitly accepts the additional risk.

', NULL, N'The control system design and implementation protects the integrity of electronically communicated information.

', NULL, N'1. The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to ensure recognition of changes to information during transmission unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures (e.g., protective distribution systems).
2. The use of cryptography within a control system will introduce latency to control system communication. The latency introduced from the use of cryptographic mechanisms must not degrade the operational performance of the control system or impact personnel safety.
3. Failure of a cryptographic mechanism must not create a denial of service. Control systems generally support the objectives of availability, integrity, and confidentiality. Therefore, the use of cryptography should be determined after careful consideration.
4. The control system maintains the integrity of information during aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Communication Integrity</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Communication integrity is one approach where electronically transmitted information is protected.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system design and implementation protects the integrity of electronically communicated information.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">If the organization is relying on a commercial service provider for communication services as a commodity item rather than a fully dedicated service, it may be more difficult to obtain the necessary assurances regarding the implementation of needed security measures for transmission integrity. When it is infeasible or impractical to obtain the necessary assurances of effective security through appropriate contracting vehicles, the organization either implements appropriate compensating security measures or explicitly accepts the additional risk.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to ensure recognition of changes to information during transmission unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures (e.g., protective distribution systems).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The use of cryptography within a control system will introduce latency to control system communication. The latency introduced from the use of cryptographic mechanisms must not degrade the operational performance of the control system or impact personnel safety.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Failure of a cryptographic mechanism must not create a denial of service. Control systems generally support the objectives of availability, integrity, and confidentiality. Therefore, the use of cryptography should be determined after careful consideration.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system maintains the integrity of information during aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-8</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 8, Annex A, Annex B.3.1.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.6</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.9', N'2.8.9', N'Communication Confidentiality', N'Comm Confidentiality', N'The control system design and implementation protects the confidentiality of communicated information where necessary.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of information during transmission unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures.
Requirement Enhancement 2 - The control system maintains the confidentiality of information during aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.', N'The use of a third-party communication service provider instead of organization-owned infrastructure may warrant the use of encryption. The use of cryptographic mechanisms within a control system could introduce communications latency because of the additional time and computing resources required to encrypt, decrypt, and authenticate each message. Any latency induced from the use of cryptographic mechanisms must not degrade the operational performance of the control system.

', NULL, N'The control system design and implementation protects the confidentiality of communicated information where necessary.

', NULL, N'1. The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of information during transmission unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures.
2. The control system maintains the confidentiality of information during aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Communication Confidentiality</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Communication confidentiality is one tactic where unauthorized information disclosure is prevented.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system design and implementation protects the confidentiality of communicated information where necessary.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The use of a third-party communication service provider instead of organization-owned infrastructure may warrant the use of encryption. The use of cryptographic mechanisms within a control system could introduce communications latency because of the additional time and computing resources required to encrypt, decrypt, and authenticate each message. Any latency induced from the use of cryptographic mechanisms must not degrade the operational performance of the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of information during transmission unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system maintains the confidentiality of information during aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-9</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-4, CC-14</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 8</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.17, App. B.3.7</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.10', N'2.8.10', N'Trusted Path', N'Trusted Path', N'The control system establishes a trusted communications path between the user and the system.', N'A trusted path is employed for high-confidence connections between the security functions of the control system and the user (e.g., for login).

Login-to-operator interface should be protected by trusted path or a compensating control. A trusted path is a mechanism by which a person at a terminal can communicate directly with the Trusted Computing Base (TCB) that provides the security functions of the system. This mechanism can only be activated by the person or the TCB and cannot be imitated by untrusted software. The TCB is the totality of protection mechanisms within a computer system including hardware, firmware, and software the combination of which is responsible for enforcing a security policy. A TCB consists of one or more components that together enforce a unified security policy over a product or system. The ability of a TCB to enforce correctly a security policy depends solely on the mechanisms within the TCB and on the correct input by system administrative personnel of parameters (e.g., a user''s clearance) related to the security policy.

', NULL, N'The control system establishes a trusted communications path between the user and the system.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Trusted Path</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Trusted path is one approach that refers to a secure connection between the system and the user.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system establishes a trusted communications path between the user and the system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">A trusted path is employed for high-confidence connections between the security functions of the control system and the user (e.g., for login).</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Login-to-operator interface should be protected by trusted path or a compensating control. A trusted path is a mechanism by which a person at a terminal can communicate directly with the Trusted Computing Base (TCB) that provides the security functions of the system. This mechanism can only be activated by the person or the TCB and cannot be imitated by untrusted software. The TCB is the totality of protection mechanisms within a computer system including hardware, firmware, and software the combination of which is responsible for enforcing a security policy. A TCB consists of one or more components that together enforce a unified security policy over a product or system. The ability of a TCB to enforce correctly a security policy depends solely on the mechanisms within the TCB and on the correct input by system administrative personnel of parameters (e.g., a user''s clearance) related to the security policy.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-11</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 8.1, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.22, App. B.3.9</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.11', N'2.8.11', N'Cryptographic Key Establishment and Management', N'Cryptographic Keys', N'When cryptography is required and employed within the control system, the organization establishes and manages cryptographic keys using automated mechanisms with supporting procedures or manual procedures.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization maintains availability of information in the event of the loss of cryptographic keys by users.', N'Organizations need to select cryptographic protection that matches the value of the information being protected and the control system operating constraints. A formal written policy needs to be developed to document the practices and procedures relating to cryptographic key establishment and management. These policies and procedures need to address, under key establishment, such items as key generation process in accordance with a specified algorithm and key sizes based on an assigned standard. Key generation must be performed using an effective random number generator. The policies for key management need to address such items as periodic key changes, key destruction, and key distribution in accordance with defined standards.

', NULL, N'When cryptography is required and employed within the control system, the organization establishes and manages cryptographic keys using automated mechanisms with supporting procedures or manual procedures.

', NULL, N'1. The organization maintains availability of information in the event of the loss of cryptographic keys by users.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Cryptographic Key Establishment and Management</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Cryptographic key establishment and management is one method and refers to procedures for managing cryptographic keys.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">When cryptography is required and employed within the control system, the organization establishes and manages cryptographic keys using automated mechanisms with supporting procedures or manual procedures.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Organizations need to select cryptographic protection that matches the value of the information being protected and the control system operating constraints. A formal written policy needs to be developed to document the practices and procedures relating to cryptographic key establishment and management. These policies and procedures need to address, under key establishment, such items as key generation process in accordance with a specified algorithm and key sizes based on an assigned standard. Key generation must be performed using an effective random number generator. The policies for key management need to address such items as periodic key changes, key destruction, and key distribution in accordance with defined standards.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization maintains availability of information in the event of the loss of cryptographic keys by users.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-12</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.9</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.12', N'2.8.12', N'Use of Validated Cryptography', N'Validated Crypto', N'The organization develops and implements a policy governing the use of cryptographic mechanisms for the protection of control system information. The organization ensures all cryptographic mechanisms comply with applicable laws, regulatory requirements, directives, policies, standards, and guidance.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - The organization protects cryptographic hardware from physical tampering and uncontrolled electronic connections.
Requirement Enhancement 2 - The organization selects cryptographic hardware with remote key management capabilities.', N'Any cryptographic modules deployed within a control system, at a minimum, must be able to meet the FIPS 140-2. Assessment of the modules must include validation of the cryptographic modules operating in approved modes of operation. The most effective safeguard is to use a cryptographic module validated by the Cryptographic Module Validation Program. Additional information on the use of validated cryptography can be found at http://csrc.nist.gov/groups/STM/cmvp/index.html.

', NULL, N'The organization develops and implements a policy governing the use of cryptographic mechanisms for the protection of control system information. The organization ensures all cryptographic mechanisms comply with applicable laws, regulatory requirements, directives, policies, standards, and guidance.

', NULL, N'1. The organization protects cryptographic hardware from physical tampering and uncontrolled electronic connections.
2. The organization selects cryptographic hardware with remote key management capabilities.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Use of Validated Cryptography</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Use of validated cryptography is one approach and means that the Cryptographic Module Validation Program has validated the cryptographic module.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops and implements a policy governing the use of cryptographic mechanisms for the protection of control system information. The organization ensures all cryptographic mechanisms comply with applicable laws, regulatory requirements, directives, policies, standards, and guidance.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Any cryptographic modules deployed within a control system, at a minimum, must be able to meet the FIPS 140-2. Assessment of the modules must include validation of the cryptographic modules operating in approved modes of operation. The most effective safeguard is to use a cryptographic module validated by the Cryptographic Module Validation Program. Additional information on the use of validated cryptography can be found at http://csrc.nist.gov/groups/STM/cmvp/index.html.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization protects cryptographic hardware from physical tampering and uncontrolled electronic connections.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization selects cryptographic hardware with remote key management capabilities.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-13</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-15</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.10</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.13', N'2.8.13', N'Collaborative Computing Devices', N'Collaborative Computing', N'The use of collaborative computing devices on the control system is strongly discouraged. If use is authorized and allowed by the organization, explicit indication of use is provided to users physically present at the devices.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - If collaborative computing devices are used on the control system, they are disconnected and powered down when not in use.
Requirement Enhancement 2 - The control system or supporting environment blocks both inbound and outbound traffic between instant messaging clients that are independently configured by end users and external service providers.
Requirement Enhancement 3 - The organization disables or removes collaborative computing devices from control systems in organization-defined secure work areas.', N'Collaborative computing devices include video and audio conferencing capabilities, networked information boards, or instant messaging technologies. Explicit indication of use includes signals to local users when cameras and microphones are activated.

', NULL, N'The use of collaborative computing devices on the control system is strongly discouraged. If use is authorized and allowed by the organization, explicit indication of use is provided to users physically present at the devices.

', NULL, N'1. If collaborative computing devices are used on the control system, they are disconnected and powered down when not in use.
2. The control system or supporting environment blocks both inbound and outbound traffic between instant messaging clients that are independently configured by end users and external service providers.
3. The organization disables or removes collaborative computing devices from control systems in organization-defined secure work areas.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Collaborative Computing Devices</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Collaborative computing devices are a vulnerability that should be avoided.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The use of collaborative computing devices on the control system is strongly discouraged. If use is authorized and allowed by the organization, explicit indication of use is provided to users physically present at the devices.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Collaborative computing devices include video and audio conferencing capabilities, networked information boards, or instant messaging technologies. Explicit indication of use includes signals to local users when cameras and microphones are activated.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">If collaborative computing devices are used on the control system, they are disconnected and powered down when not in use.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system or supporting environment blocks both inbound and outbound traffic between instant messaging clients that are independently configured by end users and external service providers.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization disables or removes collaborative computing devices from control systems in organization-defined secure work areas.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-15</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 7.15, 7.3, Annex B.3.1.4.3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.11</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.14', N'2.8.14', N'Transmission of Security Attributes', N'Security Attributes', N'The control system reliably associates security attributes (e.g., security labels and markings) with information exchanged between the enterprise systems and the control system.
Requirement Enhancement - The control system validates the integrity of security parameters exchanged between systems.', N'Security attributes may be explicitly or implicitly associated with the information contained within the control system. For example, security labels are often used in data structures to associated attributes with specific information objects such as user access privileges, nationality, or affiliation as a contractor.

', NULL, N'The control system reliably associates security attributes (e.g., security labels and markings) with information exchanged between the enterprise systems and the control system.

', NULL, N'1. The control system validates the integrity of security parameters exchanged between systems.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Transmission of Security Attributes</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Reliable transmission of security attributes is a key aspect of this protection.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system reliably associates security attributes (e.g., security labels and markings) with information exchanged between the enterprise systems and the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security attributes may be explicitly or implicitly associated with the information contained within the control system. For example, security labels are often used in data structures to associated attributes with specific information objects such as user access privileges, nationality, or affiliation as a contractor.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system validates the integrity of security parameters exchanged between systems.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-16</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.12</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.15', N'2.8.15', N'Public Key Infrastructure Certificates', N'Public Key Cert', N'The organization issues public key certificates under an appropriate certificate policy or obtains public key certificates under an appropriate certificate policy from an approved service provider.
Requirement Enhancement - Any latency induced from the use of public key certificates must not degrade the operational performance of the control system.', N'Registration to receive a public key certificate needs to include authorization by a supervisor or a responsible official and needs to be accomplished using a secure process that verifies the identity of the certificate holder and ensures that the certificate is issued to the intended party.

', NULL, N'The organization issues public key certificates under an appropriate certificate policy or obtains public key certificates under an appropriate certificate policy from an approved service provider.

', NULL, N'1. Any latency induced from the use of public key certificates must not degrade the operational performance of the control system.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Public Key Infrastructure Certificates</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Control and use of public key infrastructure certificates is a key aspect of this protection.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization issues public key certificates under an appropriate certificate policy or obtains public key certificates under an appropriate certificate policy from an approved service provider.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Registration to receive a public key certificate needs to include authorization by a supervisor or a responsible official and needs to be accomplished using a secure process that verifies the identity of the certificate holder and ensures that the certificate is issued to the intended party.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Any latency induced from the use of public key certificates must not degrade the operational performance of the control system.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-17</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.22, App. B.3.13</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.16', N'2.8.16', N'Mobile Code', N'Mobile Code', N'The organization:

1. Establishes usage restrictions and implementation guidance for mobile code technologies based on the potential to cause damage to the control system if used maliciously.
2. Documents, monitors, and manages the use of mobile code within the control system. Appropriate organizational officials authorize the use of mobile code.
Requirement Enhancement - The control system implements detection and inspection mechanisms to identify unauthorized mobile code and takes corrective actions, when necessary.', N'Mobile code technologies include Java, JavaScript, ActiveX, portable document format (PDF), Postscript, Shockwave movies, Flash animations, and VBScript. Usage restrictions and implementation guidance need to apply to both the selection and use of mobile code installed on organizational servers and mobile code downloaded and executed on individual workstations. Control procedures need to prevent the development, acquisition, or introduction of unacceptable mobile code within the control system. Additional information on risk-based approaches for the implementation of mobile code technologies can be found at http://iase.disa.mil/mcp/index.html. This link to the Mobile Code Policy and Guidance has been moved to the Department of Defense Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)-certified protected area of Information Assurance Support Environment (IASE) A person must have the proper authority for access to this document.

', NULL, N'The organization:

1. Establishes usage restrictions and implementation guidance for mobile code technologies based on the potential to cause damage to the control system if used maliciously
2. Documents, monitors, and manages the use of mobile code within the control system. Appropriate organizational officials authorize the use of mobile code.
', NULL, N'1. The control system implements detection and inspection mechanisms to identify unauthorized mobile code and takes corrective actions, when necessary.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Mobile Code</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Use of mobile code should be restricted as part of this protection.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Establishes usage restrictions and implementation guidance for mobile code technologies based on the potential to cause damage to the control system if used maliciously</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Documents, monitors, and manages the use of mobile code within the control system. Appropriate organizational officials authorize the use of mobile code.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Mobile code technologies include Java, JavaScript, ActiveX, portable document format (PDF), Postscript, Shockwave movies, Flash animations, and VBScript. Usage restrictions and implementation guidance need to apply to both the selection and use of mobile code installed on organizational servers and mobile code downloaded and executed on individual workstations. Control procedures need to prevent the development, acquisition, or introduction of unacceptable mobile code within the control system. Additional information on risk-based approaches for the implementation of mobile code technologies can be found at http://iase.disa.mil/mcp/index.html. This link to the Mobile Code Policy and Guidance has been moved to the Department of Defense Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)-certified protected area of Information Assurance Support Environment (IASE) A person must have the proper authority for access to this document.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system implements detection and inspection mechanisms to identify unauthorized mobile code and takes corrective actions, when necessary.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-18</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-5, CC-12</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.22, App. B.3.14</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.17', N'2.8.17', N'Voice-Over Internet Protocol', N'VoIP', N'The organization: (1) establishes usage restrictions and implementation guidance for VoIP technologies based on the potential to cause damage to the system if used maliciously and (2) authorizes, monitors, and controls the use of VoIP within the control system.', N'Generally, VoIP technologies should not be employed on control systems. Usage should be determined after careful consideration and after verification that it does not adversely impact the operational parameters of the ICS.

', NULL, N'The organization: (1) establishes usage restrictions and implementation guidance for VoIP technologies based on the potential to cause damage to the system if used maliciously and (2) authorizes, monitors, and controls the use of VoIP within the control system.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Voice-Over Internet Protocol</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Use of Voice over internet protocol (VoIP) should be restricted.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization: (1) establishes usage restrictions and implementation guidance for VoIP technologies based on the potential to cause damage to the system if used maliciously and (2) authorizes, monitors, and controls the use of VoIP within the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Generally, VoIP technologies should not be employed on control systems. Usage should be determined after careful consideration and after verification that it does not adversely impact the operational parameters of the ICS.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-19</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 7.3.6</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.18', N'2.8.18', N'System Connections', N'Sys Connections', N'All external control system and communication connections are identified and protected from tampering or damage.', N'External access point connections to the control system need to be secured to protect the system. Access points include any externally connected communication end point (for example, dialup modems) terminating at any device within the electronic security perimeter. The first step in securing these connections is to identify the connections along with the purpose and necessity of the connection. This information needs to be documented, tracked, and audited periodically. After identifying these connection points, the extent of their protection needs to be determined. Policies and procedures need to be developed and implemented to protect the connection to the business or enterprise system. This might include disabling the connection except when specific access is requested for a specific need, automatic timeout for the connection, etc.

', NULL, N'All external control system and communication connections are identified and protected from tampering or damage.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">System Connections</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. System connections is one tactic where control system and communication connections are identified and protected.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">All external control system and communication connections are identified and protected from tampering or damage.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">External access point connections to the control system need to be secured to protect the system. Access points include any externally connected communication end point (for example, dialup modems) terminating at any device within the electronic security perimeter. The first step in securing these connections is to identify the connections along with the purpose and necessity of the connection. This information needs to be documented, tracked, and audited periodically. After identifying these connection points, the extent of their protection needs to be determined. Policies and procedures need to be developed and implemented to protect the connection to the business or enterprise system. This might include disabling the connection except when specific access is requested for a specific need, automatic timeout for the connection, etc.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 CA-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-5</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.6, Annex B.1, B.2, B.3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 005-3. B.R1 through R1.3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.3.1.3, C.3.1.4, App. B.1.1, App. B.1.22, App. B.4.5, App. B.5.2, App. C.3.4, App. C.7, App. C.9.1, App. C.11.3</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.19', N'2.8.19', N'Security Roles', N'Security Roles', N'The control system design and implementation specifies the security roles and responsibilities for the users of the system.', N'Security roles and responsibilities for control system users need to be specified, defined, and implemented based on the sensitivity of the information handled by the user. These roles may be defined for specific job descriptions or for individuals.

', NULL, N'The control system design and implementation specifies the security roles and responsibilities for the users of the system.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Security Roles</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Security roles is one aspect of protection where user roles and responsibilities are clearly defined.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system design and implementation specifies the security roles and responsibilities for the users of the system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security roles and responsibilities for control system users need to be specified, defined, and implemented based on the sensitivity of the information handled by the user. These roles may be defined for specific job descriptions or for individuals.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 AC-5, PS-2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 1.2, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 002-3. through CIP 009-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.3.3.1.1, App. B.1.1, App. B.1.22, App. C.10.10</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.20', N'2.8.20', N'Session Authenticity', N'Session Authenticity', N'The control system provides mechanisms to protect the authenticity of device-to-device communications sessions.
Requirement Enhancement - Message authentication mechanisms should be implemented at the protocol level for both serial and routable protocols.', N'Message authentication provides protection from malformed traffic from misconfigured devices and malicious entities. The intent is to establish confidence at each end of a communications session with respect to the validity of the data and the identity of the sender. This is to address man-in-the middle attacks, which can include session hijacking, insertion of fake information, or instruction sets in the middle of a session. 

In situations where the ICS cannot protect the authenticity of communications sessions, the organization employs compensating controls (e.g., auditing measures, isolation/segmented architecture, additional physical isolation). Enhanced auditing measures or encryption mechanisms designed to enhance session authenticity must not impact ICS operations by consuming too many available resources or by slowing down communications to an unacceptable level as to constitute a self-inflicted denialofservice attack.

', NULL, N'The control system provides mechanisms to protect the authenticity of device-to-device communications sessions.

', NULL, N'1. Message authentication mechanisms should be implemented at the protocol level for both serial and routable protocols.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Session Authenticity</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Session authenticity is one method where device to device communication is protected.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system provides mechanisms to protect the authenticity of device-to-device communications sessions.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Message authentication provides protection from malformed traffic from misconfigured devices and malicious entities. The intent is to establish confidence at each end of a communications session with respect to the validity of the data and the identity of the sender. This is to address man-in-the middle attacks, which can include session hijacking, insertion of fake information, or instruction sets in the middle of a session.</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">In situations where the ICS cannot protect the authenticity of communications sessions, the organization employs compensating controls (e.g., auditing measures, isolation/segmented architecture, additional physical isolation). Enhanced auditing measures or encryption mechanisms designed to enhance session authenticity must not impact ICS operations by consuming too many available resources or by slowing down communications to an unacceptable level as to constitute a self-inflicted denialofservice attack.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Message authentication mechanisms should be implemented at the protocol level for both serial and routable protocols.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-23</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 5.9, 7.2.2, 8.1, Annex A, Annex B.1, B.2, B.3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. 3.1.1, App. B.3.6, App. B.3.18</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.21', N'2.8.21', N'Architecture and Provisioning for Name/Address Resolution Service', N'Name/Address Resolution', N'The control system devices that collectively provide name/address resolution services for an organization are fault tolerant and implement address space separation.
Requirement Enhancement - The use of secure name/address resolution services must not adversely impact the operational performance of the control system.', N'In general, do not use domain name system (DNS) services on a control system. Host-based name resolution solutions are the recommended practice. However, if DNS services are implemented, deploy at least two authoritative DNS servers. The DNS configuration on the host will reference one DNS server as the primary source and the other as the secondary source. In addition, locate the two DNS servers on different network subnets and separate geographically. If control system resources are accessible from external networks, establish authoritative DNS servers with separate address space views (internal and external) to the control system resources. The DNS server with the internal view provides name/address resolution services within the control system boundary. The DNS server with the external view only provides name/address resolution information pertaining to control system resources accessible from external resources. The list of clients who can access the authoritative DNS server with a particular view is also specified.

', NULL, N'The control system devices that collectively provide name/address resolution services for an organization are fault tolerant and implement address space separation.

', NULL, N'1. The use of secure name/address resolution services must not adversely impact the operational performance of the control system.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Architecture and Provisioning for Name/Address Resolution Service</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Architecture and provisioning for name/address resolution service is one approach where, in general, host based name resolution instead of domain name system (DNS) services are used.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system devices that collectively provide name/address resolution services for an organization are fault tolerant and implement address space separation.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">In general, do not use domain name system (DNS) services on a control system. Host-based name resolution solutions are the recommended practice. However, if DNS services are implemented, deploy at least two authoritative DNS servers. The DNS configuration on the host will reference one DNS server as the primary source and the other as the secondary source. In addition, locate the two DNS servers on different network subnets and separate geographically. If control system resources are accessible from external networks, establish authoritative DNS servers with separate address space views (internal and external) to the control system resources. The DNS server with the internal view provides name/address resolution services within the control system boundary. The DNS server with the external view only provides name/address resolution information pertaining to control system resources accessible from external resources. The list of clients who can access the authoritative DNS server with a particular view is also specified.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The use of secure name/address resolution services must not adversely impact the operational performance of the control system.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-22</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-16</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.6, App. B.3.17</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.22', N'2.8.22', N'Secure Name/Address Resolution Service (Authoritative Source)', N'Authoritative DNS', N'The control system resource (i.e., authoritative DNS server) that provides name/address resolution service provides additional artifacts (e.g., digital signatures and cryptographic keys) along with the authoritative DNS resource records it returns in response to resolution queries.
Requirement Enhancement - The control system, when operating as part of a distributed, hierarchical namespace, provides the means to indicate the security status of child subspaces and (if the child supports secure resolution services) enable verification of a chain of trust among parent and child domains.', N'In general, do not use DNS services on a control system. Host-based name resolution solutions are best practice. This requirement enables remote clients to obtain origin authentication and integrity verification assurances for the name/address resolution information obtained through the service. A DNS server is an example of control system resource that provides name/address resolution service; digital signatures and cryptographic keys are examples of additional artifacts; and DNS resource records are examples of authoritative data.

', NULL, N'The control system resource (i.e., authoritative DNS server) that provides name/address resolution service provides additional artifacts (e.g., digital signatures and cryptographic keys) along with the authoritative DNS resource records it returns in response to resolution queries.

', NULL, N'1. The control system, when operating as part of a distributed, hierarchical namespace, provides the means to indicate the security status of child subspaces and (if the child supports secure resolution services) enable verification of a chain of trust among parent and child domains.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Secure Name/Address Resolution Service (Authoritative Source)</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Secure name/address resolution service (authoritative source) is one tactic where the resolution service provides additional artifacts to enhance security.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system resource (i.e., authoritative DNS server) that provides name/address resolution service provides additional artifacts (e.g., digital signatures and cryptographic keys) along with the authoritative DNS resource records it returns in response to resolution queries.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">In general, do not use DNS services on a control system. Host-based name resolution solutions are best practice. This requirement enables remote clients to obtain origin authentication and integrity verification assurances for the name/address resolution information obtained through the service. A DNS server is an example of control system resource that provides name/address resolution service; digital signatures and cryptographic keys are examples of additional artifacts; and DNS resource records are examples of authoritative data.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system, when operating as part of a distributed, hierarchical namespace, provides the means to indicate the security status of child subspaces and (if the child supports secure resolution services) enable verification of a chain of trust among parent and child domains.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-20</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-16</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.15</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.23', N'2.8.23', N'Secure Name/Address Resolution Service (Recursive or Caching Resolver)', N'Recursive DNS', N'The control system resource (i.e., resolving or caching name server) that provides name/address resolution service for local clients performs data origin authentication and data integrity verification on the resolution responses it receives from authoritative DNS servers when requested by client systems.
Requirement Enhancement - The control system resource that implements DNS services performs data origin authentication and data integrity verification on all resolution responses whether local DNS clients (i.e., stub resolvers) explicitly request this function.', N'In general, do not use DNS services on a control system. Host-based name resolution solutions are best practice. A resolving or caching DNS server is an example of a control system resource that provides name/address resolution service for local clients, and authoritative DNS servers are examples of authoritative sources.

', NULL, N'The control system resource (i.e., resolving or caching name server) that provides name/address resolution service for local clients performs data origin authentication and data integrity verification on the resolution responses it receives from authoritative DNS servers when requested by client systems.

', NULL, N'1. The control system resource that implements DNS services performs data origin authentication and data integrity verification on all resolution responses whether local DNS clients (i.e., stub resolvers) explicitly request this function.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Secure Name/Address Resolution Service (Recursive or Caching Resolver)</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Secure name/address resolution service (recursive or caching resolver) is one method where the resolution service performs data origin authentication and data integrity verification.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system resource (i.e., resolving or caching name server) that provides name/address resolution service for local clients performs data origin authentication and data integrity verification on the resolution responses it receives from authoritative DNS servers when requested by client systems.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">In general, do not use DNS services on a control system. Host-based name resolution solutions are best practice. A resolving or caching DNS server is an example of a control system resource that provides name/address resolution service for local clients, and authoritative DNS servers are examples of authoritative sources.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system resource that implements DNS services performs data origin authentication and data integrity verification on all resolution responses whether local DNS clients (i.e., stub resolvers) explicitly request this function.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-21</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-16</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.16, App. B.3.18, App. B.4.4</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.24', N'2.8.24', N'Fail in Known State', N'Failure State', N'The control system fails to a known state for defined failures.
Requirement Enhancement - The control system preserves defined system state information in failure.', N'Failure in a known state can be interpreted by organizations in the context of safety or security in accordance with the organization’s mission/business/operational needs. Failure in a known secure state helps prevent a loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability in the event of a failure of the control system or a component of the system. Failure in a known safe state helps prevent systems from failing to a state that may cause injury to individuals or destruction to property.

', NULL, N'The control system fails to a known state for defined failures.

', NULL, N'1. The control system preserves defined system state information in failure.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Fail in Known State</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Fail in known state is one approach where the control system fails to a known, secure state.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system fails to a known state for defined failures.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Failure in a known state can be interpreted by organizations in the context of safety or security in accordance with the organization’s mission/business/operational needs. Failure in a known secure state helps prevent a loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability in the event of a failure of the control system or a component of the system. Failure in a known safe state helps prevent systems from failing to a state that may cause injury to individuals or destruction to property.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system preserves defined system state information in failure.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-24</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-14</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.17, App. B.3.22</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.25', N'2.8.25', N'Thin Nodes', N'Thin Nodes', N'The control system employs processing components that have minimal functionality and data storage.
Requirement Enhancement - Use secure data transmission media, such as fiber optic technology, to minimize data loss from eavesdropping and data tapping.', N'The deployment of control system components with minimal functionality (e.g., diskless nodes and thin client technologies) reduces the number of endpoints to be secured and may reduce the exposure of information, control systems, and services to a successful attack.

', NULL, N'The control system employs processing components that have minimal functionality and data storage.

', NULL, N'1. Use secure data transmission media, such as fiber optic technology, to minimize data loss from eavesdropping and data tapping.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Thin Nodes</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Thin nodes is one strategy where control system components have minimal functionality.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system employs processing components that have minimal functionality and data storage.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The deployment of control system components with minimal functionality (e.g., diskless nodes and thin client technologies) reduces the number of endpoints to be secured and may reduce the exposure of information, control systems, and services to a successful attack.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Use secure data transmission media, such as fiber optic technology, to minimize data loss from eavesdropping and data tapping.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-25</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.19</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.26', N'2.8.26', N'Honeypots', N'Honeypots', N'The control system includes components specifically designed to be the target of malicious attacks for the purpose of detecting, deflecting, analyzing, and tracking such attacks.
Requirement Enhancement - The control system includes components that proactively seek to identify web-based malicious code.', N'Not all ICS users should use honeypots. Only specialized entities using nonoperational equipment in highly isolated and protected zones should attempt deployment of honeypots. Created honeypots determine if active entities are attacking/probing the particular configuration the honeypot is mimicking. If deployed incorrectly, this technique can lead to a direct shortcut of established cybersecurity measures. This is a very specialized and limited application and should not be widely used. 

', NULL, N'The control system includes components specifically designed to be the target of malicious attacks for the purpose of detecting, deflecting, analyzing, and tracking such attacks.

', NULL, N'1. The control system includes components that proactively seek to identify web-based malicious code.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Honeypots</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. One approach is the use of honeypots as bait for malicious attacks.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system includes components specifically designed to be the target of malicious attacks for the purpose of detecting, deflecting, analyzing, and tracking such attacks.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Not all ICS users should use honeypots. Only specialized entities using nonoperational equipment in highly isolated and protected zones should attempt deployment of honeypots. Created honeypots determine if active entities are attacking/probing the particular configuration the honeypot is mimicking. If deployed incorrectly, this technique can lead to a direct shortcut of established cybersecurity measures. This is a very specialized and limited application and should not be widely used.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control system includes components that proactively seek to identify web-based malicious code.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-26</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-12</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.27', N'2.8.27', N'Operating System-Independent Applications', N'OS Independent Apps', N'The control system includes organization-defined applications that are independent of the operating system.', N'Operating system-independent applications are applications that can run on multiple operating systems. Such applications promote portability and reconstitution on different platform architectures, thus increasing the availability for critical functionality while an organization is under an attack exploiting vulnerabilities in a given operating system.

', NULL, N'The control system includes organization-defined applications that are independent of the operating system.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Operating System-Independent Applications</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Operating system independent applications is one approach where applications can run on multiple operating systems.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system includes organization-defined applications that are independent of the operating system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Operating system-independent applications are applications that can run on multiple operating systems. Such applications promote portability and reconstitution on different platform architectures, thus increasing the availability for critical functionality while an organization is under an attack exploiting vulnerabilities in a given operating system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-27</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.12.5</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.28', N'2.8.28', N'Confidentiality of Information at Rest', N'Confid at Rest', N'The control system protects the confidentiality of information at rest. Examples of data at rest are: configuration files and settings, alarm point setting, password files, and security filter rules.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of information at rest unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures.', N'This control is intended to address the confidentiality of information in nonmobile devices.

', NULL, N'The control system protects the confidentiality of information at rest. Examples of data at rest are: configuration files and settings, alarm point setting, password files, and security filter rules.

', NULL, N'1. The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of information at rest unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Confidentiality of Information at Rest</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Confidentiality of information at rest is one strategy where stable, unchanging files and settings are protected.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system protects the confidentiality of information at rest. Examples of data at rest are: configuration files and settings, alarm point setting, password files, and security filter rules.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">This control is intended to address the confidentiality of information in nonmobile devices.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure of information at rest unless otherwise protected by alternative physical measures.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-28</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-15</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.20</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.29', N'2.8.29', N'Heterogeneity', N'Heterogeneity', N'The organization employs diverse technologies in the implementation of the control system.', N'Increasing the diversity of technologies within the control system reduces the impact from the exploitation of a specific technology.

', NULL, N'The organization employs diverse technologies in the implementation of the control system.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Heterogeneity</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Heterogeneity is one approach that means diversity of technologies.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization employs diverse technologies in the implementation of the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Increasing the diversity of technologies within the control system reduces the impact from the exploitation of a specific technology.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-29</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.21</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.30', N'2.8.30', N'Virtualization Techniques', N'Virtualization Tech', N'The organization employs virtualization techniques to present gateway components into control systems environments as other types of components, or components with differing configurations.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - The organization employs virtualization techniques to deploy a diversity of operating systems environments and applications.
Requirement Enhancement 2 - The organization changes the diversity of operating systems and applications on an organization-defined frequency.
Requirement Enhancement 3 - The organization employs randomness in the implementation of the virtualization.', N'Virtualization techniques provide organizations with the ability to disguise gateway components into control systems environments, potentially reducing the likelihood of successful attacks without the cost of having multiple platforms.

', NULL, N'The organization employs virtualization techniques to present gateway components into control systems environments as other types of components or components with differing configurations.

', NULL, N'1. The organization employs virtualization techniques to deploy a diversity of operating systems environments and applications.
2. The organization changes the diversity of operating systems and applications on an organization-defined frequency.
3. The organization employs randomness in the implementation of the virtualization.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Virtualization Techniques</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Virtualization techniques is one method where gateway components are disguised.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization employs virtualization techniques to present gateway components into control systems environments as other types of components or components with differing configurations.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Virtualization techniques provide organizations with the ability to disguise gateway components into control systems environments, potentially reducing the likelihood of successful attacks without the cost of having multiple platforms.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs virtualization techniques to deploy a diversity of operating systems environments and applications.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization changes the diversity of operating systems and applications on an organization-defined frequency.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs randomness in the implementation of the virtualization.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-30</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-5</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.31', N'2.8.31', N'Covert Channel Analysis', N'Covert Channels', N'The organization requires that control system developers/integrators perform covert channel analysis to identify those aspects of system communication that are potential avenues for covert storage and timing channels.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization tests a subset of the vendor-identified covert channel avenues to determine if they are exploitable.', N'Control system developers/integrators are in the best position to identify potential avenues within the system that might lead to covert channels. Covert channel analysis is a meaningful activity when the potential exists for unauthorized information flows across security domains in the case of control systems containing export controlled information and having connections to the Internet.

', NULL, N'The organization requires that control system developers/integrators perform covert channel analysis to identify those aspects of system communication that are potential avenues for covert storage and timing channels.

', NULL, N'1. The organization tests a subset of the vendor-identified covert channel avenues to determine if they are exploitable.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Covert Channel Analysis</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Covert channel analysis is one approach where developers identify potential attack avenues.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization requires that control system developers/integrators perform covert channel analysis to identify those aspects of system communication that are potential avenues for covert storage and timing channels.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Control system developers/integrators are in the best position to identify potential avenues within the system that might lead to covert channels. Covert channel analysis is a meaningful activity when the potential exists for unauthorized information flows across security domains in the case of control systems containing export controlled information and having connections to the Internet.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization tests a subset of the vendor-identified covert channel avenues to determine if they are exploitable.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-31</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-5</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.32', N'2.8.32', N'Information System Partitioning', N'Info Sys Partitioning', N'The organization partitions the information system into components residing in separate physical domains (or environments) as necessary.', N'Information system partitioning is a part of a defense-in-depth protection strategy. An organizational assessment of risk guides the partitioning of information system components into separate physical domains (or environments). The security categorization also guides the selection of appropriate candidates for domain partitioning when system components can be associated with different system impact levels derived from the categorizations. Managed interfaces restrict or prohibit network access and information flow among partitioned information system components.

', NULL, N'The organization partitions the information system into components residing in separate physical domains (or environments) as necessary.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information System Partitioning</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Information system partitioning is one strategy where information system components are separated into different environments.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization partitions the information system into components residing in separate physical domains (or environments) as necessary.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information system partitioning is a part of a defense-in-depth protection strategy. An organizational assessment of risk guides the partitioning of information system components into separate physical domains (or environments). The security categorization also guides the selection of appropriate candidates for domain partitioning when system components can be associated with different system impact levels derived from the categorizations. Managed interfaces restrict or prohibit network access and information flow among partitioned information system components.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-32</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.2.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.33', N'2.8.33', N'Transmission Preparation Integrity', N'Tx Prep Integrity', N'The control system protects the integrity of information during the processes of data aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.', N'Information can be subjected to unauthorized changes (i.e., malicious and unintentional modification) at information aggregation or protocol transformation points.

', NULL, N'The information system protects the integrity of information during the processes of data aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Transmission Preparation Integrity</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. One approach is where information transmission is recognized as a vulnerability, and more secure preparation is employed.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The information system protects the integrity of information during the processes of data aggregation, packaging, and transformation in preparation for transmission.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information can be subjected to unauthorized changes (i.e., malicious and unintentional modification) at information aggregation or protocol transformation points.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-33</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.6</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.8.34', N'2.8.34', N'Non-Modifiable Executable Programs', N'Non-Mod Exec Prgrms', N'The information system:

1. Loads and executes the operating system software from hardware-enforced, read-only media.
2. Loads and executes authorized applications from hardware-enforced, read-only media.
Requirement Enhancements 1 - The organization employs system components with no writable storage that is persistent across component restart or power on/off cycles.
Requirement Enhancements 2 - The organization protects the integrity of the information on read-only media.', N'In this control, operating system software is defined as the base code on which applications are hosted. Hardware-enforced, read-only media include CD-R/DVD-Rs. The use of nonmodifiable storage media ensures the integrity of the software from the point of creation as a read-only image.

', NULL, N'The information system:

1. Loads and executes the operating system software from hardware-enforced, read-only media
2. Loads and executes authorized applications from hardware-enforced, read-only media.
', NULL, N'1.	The organization employs system components with no writable storage that is persistent across component restart or power on/off cycles.

    Enhanced Supplemental Guidance This control enhancement eliminates the possibility of malicious code insertion via persistent, writeable storage within the designated information system component and requires that no removable storage be employed.

2.	The Organization protects the integrity of the information on read-only media.

    Enhanced Supplemental Guidance This control enhancement covers protection of the integrity of information placed on read-only media, controlling the media after information has been recorded onto the media. Measures may include a combination of prevention and detection/response.', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Non-Modifiable Executable Programs</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System and communication protection consists of steps taken to protect the control system and the communication links between system components from cyber intrusions. Nonmodifiable executable programs is one approach where read only media is used to the extent possible.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The information system:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Loads and executes the operating system software from hardware-enforced, read-only media</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Loads and executes authorized applications from hardware-enforced, read-only media.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">In this control, operating system software is defined as the base code on which applications are hosted. Hardware-enforced, read-only media include CD-R/DVD-Rs. The use of nonmodifiable storage media ensures the integrity of the software from the point of creation as a read-only image.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs system components with no writable storage that is persistent across component restart or power on/off cycles.</Paragraph></ListItem></List><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Enhanced Supplemental Guidance This control enhancement eliminates the possibility of malicious code insertion via persistent, writeable storage within the designated information system component and requires that no removable storage be employed.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></ListItem><ListItem><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The Organization protects the integrity of the information on read-only media.</Paragraph></ListItem></List><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Enhanced Supplemental Guidance This control enhancement covers protection of the integrity of information placed on read-only media, controlling the media after information has been recorded onto the media. Measures may include a combination of prevention and detection/response.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-34</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.1', N'2.9.1', N'Information and Document Management Policy and Procedures', N'Doc Mgmt Policy/Proced', N'The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, control system information and document management policy that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance.
2. Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the control system information and document management policy and associated system maintenance controls.
', N'The organization ensures the control system information and document management policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The control system information and document management policy can be included as part of the general information security policy for the organization. System information and document management procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for a particular control system when required.

', NULL, N'The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, control system information and document management policy that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance
2. Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the control system information and document management policy and associated system maintenance controls.
', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information and Document Management Policy and Procedures</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management policies are an extension of higher-level organizational security policies. Procedures implement the policies and allow the organization to communicate its security-related requirements to employees, third-party contractors, and vendors.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">A formal, documented, control system information and document management policy that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the control system information and document management policy and associated system maintenance controls.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization ensures the control system information and document management policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The control system information and document management policy can be included as part of the general information security policy for the organization. System information and document management procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for a particular control system when required.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SI-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 002-3. through CIP 009-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.3.1, App. C.11</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.2', N'2.9.2', N'Information and Document Retention', N'Info & Doc Retention', N'The organization manages control system-related data, including establishing retention policies and procedures for both electronic and paper data and manages access to the data based on formally assigned roles and responsibilities.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization performs legal reviews of the retention policies to ensure compliance with all applicable laws and regulations.', N'The organization develops policies and procedures detailing the retention of company information. These procedures address retention/destruction issues for all applicable information media. Any legal or regulatory requirements are considered when developing these policies and procedures. Information associated with the development and execution of a control system is important, sensitive, and needs to be appropriately managed. The National Archives and Records Administration provides guidance on records retention.

', NULL, N'The organization manages control system-related data, including establishing retention policies and procedures for both electronic and paper data and manages access to the data based on formally assigned roles and responsibilities.

', NULL, N'1. The organization performs legal reviews of the retention policies to ensure compliance with all applicable laws and regulations.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information and Document Retention</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Information and document retention is one aspect of information and document management and concerns developing retention policies and establishing access protocols.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization manages control system-related data, including establishing retention policies and procedures for both electronic and paper data and manages access to the data based on formally assigned roles and responsibilities.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization develops policies and procedures detailing the retention of company information. These procedures address retention/destruction issues for all applicable information media. Any legal or regulatory requirements are considered when developing these policies and procedures. Information associated with the development and execution of a control system is important, sensitive, and needs to be appropriately managed. The National Archives and Records Administration provides guidance on records retention.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization performs legal reviews of the retention policies to ensure compliance with all applicable laws and regulations.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SI-12</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3.B.R4</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.5, App. B.2.11, App. C.3.4, App. C.3.10</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.3', N'2.9.3', N'Information Handling', N'Info Handling', N'Organization-implemented policies and procedures detailing the handling of information are developed and periodically reviewed and updated.', N'Written policies and procedures detail access, sharing, copying, transmittal, distribution, and disposal or destruction of control system information. These policies or procedures include the periodic review of all information to ensure it is properly handled. The organization protects information against unauthorized access, misuse, or corruption during transportation or transmission. The organization distributes or shares information on a need-to-know basis and considers legal and regulatory requirements when developing these policies and procedures.

', NULL, N'Organization implemented policies and procedures detailing the handling of information are developed and periodically reviewed and updated.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information Handling</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Information handling is one aspect of information and document management. It concerns developing policies detailing how the information will be protected against unauthorized access, misuse, or corruption.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Organization implemented policies and procedures detailing the handling of information are developed and periodically reviewed and updated.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Written policies and procedures detail access, sharing, copying, transmittal, distribution, and disposal or destruction of control system information. These policies or procedures include the periodic review of all information to ensure it is properly handled. The organization protects information against unauthorized access, misuse, or corruption during transportation or transmission. The organization distributes or shares information on a need-to-know basis and considers legal and regulatory requirements when developing these policies and procedures.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 MP-1, SI-12</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 002-3. B.R4.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.1, App. C.1.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.4', N'2.9.4', N'Information Classification', N'Info Classification', N'All information is classified to indicate the protection required commensurate with its sensitivity and consequence.', N'A minimum of three levels of classification should be defined for control system information to indicate the protection required commensurate with its sensitivity and consequence. These levels may be company proprietary, restricted, or public, indicating the need, priority, and level of protection required for that information. These information classification levels provide guidance for access and control to include sharing, copying, transmittal, and distribution appropriate for the level of protection required.

', NULL, N'All information is classified to indicate the protection required commensurate with its sensitivity and consequence.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information Classification</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Information classification is one aspect of information and document management that includes grading the information to establish protection requirements.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">All information is classified to indicate the protection required commensurate with its sensitivity and consequence.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">A minimum of three levels of classification should be defined for control system information to indicate the protection required commensurate with its sensitivity and consequence. These levels may be company proprietary, restricted, or public, indicating the need, priority, and level of protection required for that information. These information classification levels provide guidance for access and control to include sharing, copying, transmittal, and distribution appropriate for the level of protection required.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 RA-2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-9</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 6, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3. B.R4.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.8.4</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.5', N'2.9.5', N'Information Exchange', N'Info Exchange', N'Formal contractual and confidentiality agreements are established for the exchange of information and software between the organization and external parties.
Requirement Enhancement - If a specific device needs to communicate with another device outside the control system network, communications need to be limited to only the devices that need to communicate. All other ports and routes need to be locked down or disabled.', N'When it is necessary for the control system to communicate information to another organization or external party system, the operators need to mutually develop a formal contractual and confidentiality agreement and use a secure method of communication. These formal exchange policies, procedures, and security controls need to be in place to protect the exchange of information using all types of communication facilities.

', NULL, N'Formal contractual and confidentiality agreements are established for the exchange of information and software between the organization and external parties.

', NULL, N'1. If a specific device needs to communicate with another device outside the control system network, communications need to be limited to only the devices that need to communicate. All other ports and routes need to be locked down or disabled.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information Exchange</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Information exchange is one aspect of information and document management and concerns establishing procedures to securely communicate information to external parties.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Formal contractual and confidentiality agreements are established for the exchange of information and software between the organization and external parties.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">When it is necessary for the control system to communicate information to another organization or external party system, the operators need to mutually develop a formal contractual and confidentiality agreement and use a secure method of communication. These formal exchange policies, procedures, and security controls need to be in place to protect the exchange of information using all types of communication facilities.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">If a specific device needs to communicate with another device outside the control system network, communications need to be limited to only the devices that need to communicate. All other ports and routes need to be locked down or disabled.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SC-16</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3. B.R5</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.12</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.6', N'2.9.6', N'Information and Document Classification', N'Info/Doc Classification', N'The organization develops policies and procedures to classify data, including establishing:

1. Retention policies and procedures for both electronic and paper media.
2. Classification policies and methods (e.g., restricted, classified, general).
3. Access and control policies, to include sharing, copying, transmittal, and distribution appropriate for the level of protection required.
4. Access to the data based on formally assigned roles and responsibilities for the control system.
Requirement Enhancement - The organization periodically reviews information that requires special control or handling to determine whether such special handling is still required.', N'Companies use both comprehensive information and document management policies for their cybersecurity management system. Inherent in this is an information classification system that allows information assets to receive the appropriate level of protection. The organization defines information classification levels (e.g., restricted, classified, general) for access and control to include sharing, copying, transmittal, and distribution appropriate for the level of protection required. The organization also classifies all information (i.e., control system design information, network diagrams, process programs, vulnerability assessments) to indicate the need, priority, and level of protection required commensurate with its sensitivity and consequence.

', NULL, N'The organization develops policies and procedures to classify data, including establishing:

1. Retention policies and procedures for both electronic and paper media
2. Classification policies and methods (e.g., restricted, classified, general)
3. Access and control policies, to include sharing, copying, transmittal, and distribution appropriate for the level of protection required
4. Access to the data based on formally assigned roles and responsibilities for the control system.
', NULL, N'1. The organization periodically reviews information that requires special control or handling to determine whether such special handling is still required.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information and Document Classification</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Information and document classification is one aspect of information and document management and deals with developing policies for retention, access control, and classification methods.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops policies and procedures to classify data, including establishing:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Retention policies and procedures for both electronic and paper media</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Classification policies and methods (e.g., restricted, classified, general)</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Access and control policies, to include sharing, copying, transmittal, and distribution appropriate for the level of protection required</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Access to the data based on formally assigned roles and responsibilities for the control system.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Companies use both comprehensive information and document management policies for their cybersecurity management system. Inherent in this is an information classification system that allows information assets to receive the appropriate level of protection. The organization defines information classification levels (e.g., restricted, classified, general) for access and control to include sharing, copying, transmittal, and distribution appropriate for the level of protection required. The organization also classifies all information (i.e., control system design information, network diagrams, process programs, vulnerability assessments) to indicate the need, priority, and level of protection required commensurate with its sensitivity and consequence.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization periodically reviews information that requires special control or handling to determine whether such special handling is still required.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 AC-1, AC-3, MP-1, MP-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-9</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 6, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3. B.R4.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.3.1, App. C.1.3</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.7', N'2.9.7', N'Information and Document Retrieval', N'Info/Doc Retrieval', N'The organization develops policies and procedures that provide details of the retrieval of written and electronic records, equipment, and other media for the control system in the overall information and document management policy.', N'The organization employs appropriate measures to ensure long-term records information can be retrieved (i.e., converting the data to a newer format, retaining older equipment that can read the data). Any legal or regulatory requirements are considered when developing these policies and procedures. The organization takes special care to confirm the security, availability, and usability of the control system configuration, which includes the logic used in developing the configuration or programming for the life of the control system.

', NULL, N'The organization develops policies and procedures that provide details of the retrieval of written and electronic records, equipment, and other media for the control system in the overall information and document management policy.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information and Document Retrieval</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Information and document retrieval is one aspect of information and document management and concerns how the information will be maintained in a retrievable form.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops policies and procedures that provide details of the retrieval of written and electronic records, equipment, and other media for the control system in the overall information and document management policy.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs appropriate measures to ensure long-term records information can be retrieved (i.e., converting the data to a newer format, retaining older equipment that can read the data). Any legal or regulatory requirements are considered when developing these policies and procedures. The organization takes special care to confirm the security, availability, and usability of the control system configuration, which includes the logic used in developing the configuration or programming for the life of the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 AC-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.1.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.8', N'2.9.8', N'Information and Document Destruction', N'Info/Doc Destruction', N'The organization develops policies and procedures detailing the destruction of written and electronic records, equipment, and other media for the control system, without compromising the confidentiality of the data.', N'The organization develops policies and procedures detailing the destruction and disposal of written and electronic records, equipment, and other media in the overall information and document management policy. This also includes the method of disposal such as shredding of paper records, erasing of disks or other electronic media, or physical destruction. All legal or regulatory requirements need to be considered when developing these policies and procedures.

', NULL, N'The organization develops policies and procedures detailing the destruction of written and electronic records, equipment, and other media for the control system, without compromising the confidentiality of the data.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information and Document Destruction</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Information and document destruction is one aspect of information and document management and deals with how the information media will be disposed to prevent unauthorized access.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops policies and procedures detailing the destruction of written and electronic records, equipment, and other media for the control system, without compromising the confidentiality of the data.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization develops policies and procedures detailing the destruction and disposal of written and electronic records, equipment, and other media in the overall information and document management policy. This also includes the method of disposal such as shredding of paper records, erasing of disks or other electronic media, or physical destruction. All legal or regulatory requirements need to be considered when developing these policies and procedures.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.1.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.9', N'2.9.9', N'Information and Document Management Review', N'Info/Doc Mgmt Review', N'The organization performs periodic reviews of compliance with the control system information and document security management policy to ensure compliance with any laws and regulatory requirements.', N'The organization periodically reviews compliance in the information and document management security policy. The compliance review procedure needs to consider all legal and regulatory documentation requirements applicable to the control system.

', NULL, N'The organization performs periodic reviews of compliance with the control system information and document security management policy to ensure compliance with any laws and regulatory requirements.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Information and Document Management Review</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Information and document management review is one aspect of information and document management and requires periodic compliance reviews.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization performs periodic reviews of compliance with the control system information and document security management policy to ensure compliance with any laws and regulatory requirements.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization periodically reviews compliance in the information and document management security policy. The compliance review procedure needs to consider all legal and regulatory documentation requirements applicable to the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 1.2, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 003-3. D</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.2.3, App. C.1.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.10', N'2.9.10', N'Media Marking - Info Management', N'Media Marking', N'The organization:

1. Marks, in accordance with organizational policies and procedures, removable system media and system output indicating the distribution limitations, handling caveats, and applicable security markings (if any) of the information.
2. Exempts an organization-defined list of media types or hardware components from marking as long as the exempted items remain within the organization-defined protected environment (e.g., controlled areas).', N'The term marking is distinguished from the term labeling. Marking is used in security controls when referring to information that is human-readable. The term labeling is used in the context of marking internal data structures within the system for access control purposes for information in process, in storage, or in transit. Removable system media include both digital media (e.g., magnetic tapes, external/removable hard drives, flash/thumb drives, CDs, DVDs, diskettes) and nondigital media (e.g., paper, microfilm). An organizational assessment of risk guides the selection of media requiring marking. The rigor with which this control is applied is commensurate with the FIPS 199 security categorization of the information contained on the media. For example, marking is not required for media containing information determined by the organization to be in the public domain or to be publicly releasable. Some organizations may require marking for public information indicating that the information is publicly releasable. Organizations may extend the scope of this control to include information system output devices containing organizational information including monitors and printers. Marking of removable media and information system output is consistent with applicable laws, Executive orders, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance.

', NULL, N'The organization:

1. Marks, in accordance with organizational policies and procedures, removable system media and system output indicating the distribution limitations, handling caveats, and applicable security markings (if any) of the information
2. Exempts an organization-defined list of media types or hardware components from marking as long as the exempted items remain within the organization-defined protected environment.
', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Media Marking - Info Management</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Media marking is one aspect of information and document management that concerns marking (as opposed to labeling) human readable information.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Marks, in accordance with organizational policies and procedures, removable system media and system output indicating the distribution limitations, handling caveats, and applicable security markings (if any) of the information</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Exempts an organization-defined list of media types or hardware components from marking as long as the exempted items remain within the organization-defined protected environment.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The term marking is distinguished from the term labeling. Marking is used in security controls when referring to information that is human-readable. The term labeling is used in the context of marking internal data structures within the system for access control purposes for information in process, in storage, or in transit. Removable system media include both digital media (e.g., magnetic tapes, external/removable hard drives, flash/thumb drives, CDs, DVDs, diskettes) and nondigital media (e.g., paper, microfilm). An organizational assessment of risk guides the selection of media requiring marking. The rigor with which this control is applied is commensurate with the FIPS 199 security categorization of the information contained on the media. For example, marking is not required for media containing information determined by the organization to be in the public domain or to be publicly releasable. Some organizations may require marking for public information indicating that the information is publicly releasable. Organizations may extend the scope of this control to include information system output devices containing organizational information including monitors and printers. Marking of removable media and information system output is consistent with applicable laws, Executive orders, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 MP-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-9</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.1.3</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.9.11', N'2.9.11', N'Security Attributes', N'Security Attributes', N'The control system supports and maintains the binding of user-defined security attributes to information in storage, in process, and in transmission in accordance with:

a) Access control requirements.
b) Special dissemination, handling, or distribution instructions.
c) The system security policy.
Requirement Enhancements 1 - The information system dynamically reconfigures security attributes in accordance with an identified security policy as information is created and combined.
Requirement Enhancements 2 - The information system allows authorized entities to change security attributes.
Requirement Enhancements 3 - The information system maintains the binding of security attributes to information with sufficient assurance that the information attribute association can be used as the basis for automated policy actions.
Requirement Enhancements 4 - The information system allows authorized users to associate security attributes with information.
Requirement Enhancements 5 - The information system displays security attributes in human-readable form on each object-output from the system-to-system output devices to identify special dissemination, handling, or distribution instructions.', N'Security attributes are specified characteristics used on internal data structures to enable the implementation of access control, flow control, special dissemination, handling, or distribution instructions or to support other aspects of the information security policy. The term security label is often used to associate a set of security attributes with a specific information object as part of the data structure for that object (e.g., user access privileges, nationality, and contractor affiliation). Such labels are often used to implement access control and flow control policies.

', NULL, N'The control system supports and maintains the binding of user-defined security attributes to information in storage, in process, and in transmission in accordance with:

1. Access control requirements
2. Special dissemination, handling, or distribution instructions
3. Otherwise, as required by the system security policy.
', NULL, N'1.	The information system dynamically reconfigures security attributes in accordance with an identified security policy as information is created and combined.

2.	The information system allows authorized entities to change security attributes.
3.	The information system maintains the binding of security attributes to information with sufficient assurance that the information attribute association can be used as the basis for automated policy actions.

    Enhanced Supplemental Guidance Examples of automated policy actions include automated access control decisions (e.g., mandatory Access Control decisions) or decisions to release/or not release information (e.g., information flows via cross domain systems).

4.	The information system allows authorized users to associate security attributes with information

    Enhanced Supplemental Guidance The support provided by the information system can vary from prompting users to select security attributes to be associated with specific information objects, to ensure that the combination of attributes selected is valid.

5.	The information system displays security attributes in human-readable form on each object-output from the system-to-system output devices to identify special dissemination, handling, or distribution instructions.

    Enhanced Supplemental Guidance Objects output from the information system include pages, screens, or equivalent. Output devices include printers, video displays on computer terminals, monitors, screens on HMIs, notebooks/laptop computer, and personal digital assistants.', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Security Attributes</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Information and document management ensures that important and sensitive digital and hardcopy information associated with the development and execution of a control system is appropriately managed and protected. Security attributes is one aspect of information and document management and requires the control system to support security labels.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The control system supports and maintains the binding of user-defined security attributes to information in storage, in process, and in transmission in accordance with:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Access control requirements</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Special dissemination, handling, or distribution instructions</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Otherwise, as required by the system security policy.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security attributes are specified characteristics used on internal data structures to enable the implementation of access control, flow control, special dissemination, handling, or distribution instructions or to support other aspects of the information security policy. The term security label is often used to associate a set of security attributes with a specific information object as part of the data structure for that object (e.g., user access privileges, nationality, and contractor affiliation). Such labels are often used to implement access control and flow control policies.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The information system dynamically reconfigures security attributes in accordance with an identified security policy as information is created and combined.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The information system allows authorized entities to change security attributes.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The information system maintains the binding of security attributes to information with sufficient assurance that the information attribute association can be used as the basis for automated policy actions.</Paragraph></ListItem></List><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Enhanced Supplemental Guidance Examples of automated policy actions include automated access control decisions (e.g., mandatory Access Control decisions) or decisions to release/or not release information (e.g., information flows via cross domain systems).</Paragraph></ListItem></List></ListItem><ListItem><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The information system allows authorized users to associate security attributes with information</Paragraph></ListItem></List><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Enhanced Supplemental Guidance The support provided by the information system can vary from prompting users to select security attributes to be associated with specific information objects, to ensure that the combination of attributes selected is valid.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></ListItem><ListItem><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The information system displays security attributes in human-readable form on each object-output from the system-to-system output devices to identify special dissemination, handling, or distribution instructions.</Paragraph></ListItem></List><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Enhanced Supplemental Guidance Objects output from the information system include pages, screens, or equivalent. Output devices include printers, video displays on computer terminals, monitors, screens on HMIs, notebooks/laptop computer, and personal digital assistants.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 AC-16</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.1.3</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.1', N'2.10.1', N'System Maintenance Policy and Procedures', N'Maint Policy/Proced', N'The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, control system maintenance policy that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance.
2. Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the control system maintenance policy and associated system maintenance controls.', N'The organization ensures the control system maintenance policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The control system maintenance policy can be included as part of the general information security policy for the organization. System maintenance procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for a particular control system when required.

', NULL, N'The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:

1. A formal, documented, control system maintenance policy that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance
2. Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the control system maintenance policy and associated system maintenance controls.
', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">System Maintenance Policy and Procedures</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">System maintenance policies are an extension of higher-level organizational security policies. Procedures implement the policies and allow the organization to communicate their security-related requirements to employees, third-party contractors, and vendors.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops, disseminates, and periodically reviews and updates:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">A formal, documented, control system maintenance policy that addresses purpose, scope, roles, responsibilities, management commitment, coordination among organizational entities, and compliance</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Formal, documented procedures to facilitate the implementation of the control system maintenance policy and associated system maintenance controls.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization ensures the control system maintenance policy and procedures are consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. The control system maintenance policy can be included as part of the general information security policy for the organization. System maintenance procedures can be developed for the security program in general and for a particular control system when required.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 MA-1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.6, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 007-3. A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.3.3.2.4, C.4, App. C.4.1</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.2', N'2.10.2', N'Legacy System Upgrades', N'Legacy Sys Upgrades', N'The organization develops policies and procedures to upgrade existing legacy control systems to include security mitigating measures commensurate with the organization''s risk tolerance and the risk to the system and processes controlled.', N'Legacy systems are those control systems currently in place for control of the organization’s processes. In some cases, these systems were installed before a concern about system security existed, and hence, security mitigation measures were not included. The organization determines the current configuration of the control system and then provides system upgrades as required to meet the organization’s security requirements.

', NULL, N'The organization develops policies and procedures to upgrade existing legacy control systems to include security mitigating measures commensurate with the organization’s risk tolerance and the risk to the system and processes controlled.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Legacy System Upgrades</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security is most effective when it is designed into the control system at the earliest stages of development and sustained, using effective maintenance, throughout the life cycle of the system and through all future configurations. Legacy system upgrade is an aspect of system development and maintenance that deals with security mitigation for older or existing control systems.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization develops policies and procedures to upgrade existing legacy control systems to include security mitigating measures commensurate with the organization’s risk tolerance and the risk to the system and processes controlled.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Legacy systems are those control systems currently in place for control of the organization’s processes. In some cases, these systems were installed before a concern about system security existed, and hence, security mitigation measures were not included. The organization determines the current configuration of the control system and then provides system upgrades as required to meet the organization’s security requirements.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 SA-8</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-7, CC-16</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition Annex B.3.1.6.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.12.4</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.3', N'2.10.3', N'System Monitoring and Evaluation', N'Monitor/Eval', N'The organization conducts periodic security vulnerability assessments according to the risk management plan. The control system is then updated to address any identified vulnerabilities in accordance with organization''s control system maintenance policy.', N'Control systems need to be monitored and evaluated according to the risk management plan periodically to identify vulnerabilities or conditions that might affect the security of a control system. The frequency of these evaluations needs to be based on the organization''s risk mitigation policy. Changing security requirements and vulnerabilities necessitate a system review. These reviews need to be carefully planned and documented in accordance with the organization configuration management policy to identify any changes to the system. The organization maintains contact with other organizations that have similar systems to determine changing vulnerabilities.

', NULL, N'The organization conducts periodic security vulnerability assessments according to the risk management plan. The control system is then updated to address any identified vulnerabilities in accordance with organization’s control system maintenance policy.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">System Monitoring and Evaluation</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security is most effective when it is designed into the control system at the earliest stages of development and sustained, using effective maintenance, throughout the life cycle of the system and through all future configurations. System monitoring and evaluation is an aspect of system development and maintenance that requires security vulnerability assessments to be conducted.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization conducts periodic security vulnerability assessments according to the risk management plan. The control system is then updated to address any identified vulnerabilities in accordance with organization’s control system maintenance policy.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Control systems need to be monitored and evaluated according to the risk management plan periodically to identify vulnerabilities or conditions that might affect the security of a control system. The frequency of these evaluations needs to be based on the organization''s risk mitigation policy. Changing security requirements and vulnerabilities necessitate a system review. These reviews need to be carefully planned and documented in accordance with the organization configuration management policy to identify any changes to the system. The organization maintains contact with other organizations that have similar systems to determine changing vulnerabilities.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 CA-2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-17</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.3, Annex B.2.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 007-3. B.R6</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.4.1.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.4', N'2.10.4', N'Backup and Recovery', N'Backup/Recovery', N'The organization makes and secures backups of critical system software, applications, and data for use if the control system operating system software becomes corrupted or destroyed.', N'Control system operating software may be compromised due to an incident or disaster. A copy of the operating system software needs to be made, updated regularly, and stored in a secure environment so that it can be used to restore the control system to normal operations. In many instances, a backup control site can serve this purpose.

', NULL, N'The organization makes and secures backups of critical system software, applications, and data for use if the control system operating system software becomes corrupted or destroyed.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Backup and Recovery</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security is most effective when it is designed into the control system at the earliest stages of development and sustained, using effective maintenance, throughout the life cycle of the system and through all future configurations. Backup and recovery is an aspect of system development and maintenance that deals with the creation and protection of system backups.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization makes and secures backups of critical system software, applications, and data for use if the control system operating system software becomes corrupted or destroyed.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Control system operating software may be compromised due to an incident or disaster. A copy of the operating system software needs to be made, updated regularly, and stored in a secure environment so that it can be used to restore the control system to normal operations. In many instances, a backup control site can serve this purpose.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 CP-6, CP-10</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-13</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.4, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 009-3 B.R4</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.8.1, App. C.9.1, App. C.9.5, App. C.9.6, App. C.9.7</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.5', N'2.10.5', N'Unplanned System Maintenance', N'Unplanned Maint', N'The organization reviews and follows security requirements for a control system before undertaking any unplanned maintenance activities of control system components (including field devices). Documentation includes the following:

a) The date and time of maintenance.
b) The name of the individual(s) performing the maintenance.
c) The name of the escort, if necessary.
d) A description of the maintenance performed.
e) A list of equipment removed or replaced (including identification numbers, if applicable).
Requirement Enhancement - The organization documents the decision and justification should unplanned maintenance not be performed on a control system after the identification of a security vulnerability.', N'Unplanned maintenance is required to support control system operation in the event of system/component malfunction or failure. Security requirements necessitate that all unplanned maintenance activities use approved contingency plans and document all actions taken to restore operability to the system.

', NULL, N'The organization reviews and follows security requirements for a control system before undertaking any unplanned maintenance activities of control system components (including field devices). Documentation includes the following:

1. The date and time of maintenance
2. The name of the individual(s) performing the maintenance
3. The name of the escort, if necessary
4. A description of the maintenance performed
5. A list of equipment removed or replaced (including identification numbers, if applicable).
', NULL, N'1. The organization documents the decision and justification should unplanned maintenance not be performed on a control system after the identification of a security vulnerability.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Unplanned System Maintenance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security is most effective when it is designed into the control system at the earliest stages of development and sustained, using effective maintenance, throughout the life cycle of the system and through all future configurations. Unplanned system maintenance is an aspect of system development and maintenance that requires comprehensive planning and documentation should unplanned maintenance be needed.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization reviews and follows security requirements for a control system before undertaking any unplanned maintenance activities of control system components (including field devices). Documentation includes the following:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The date and time of maintenance</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The name of the individual(s) performing the maintenance</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The name of the escort, if necessary</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">A description of the maintenance performed</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">A list of equipment removed or replaced (including identification numbers, if applicable).</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Unplanned maintenance is required to support control system operation in the event of system/component malfunction or failure. Security requirements necessitate that all unplanned maintenance activities use approved contingency plans and document all actions taken to restore operability to the system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization documents the decision and justification should unplanned maintenance not be performed on a control system after the identification of a security vulnerability.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.4, 3.8, Annex A, Annex B.3.1</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.3.11, App. C.4.1</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.6', N'2.10.6', N'Periodic System Maintenance', N'Periodic Maint', N'The organization:

1. Schedules, performs, documents, and reviews records of maintenance and repairs on system components in accordance with manufacturer or vendor specifications and/or organizational requirements.
2. Explicitly approves the removal of the system or system components from organizational facilities for offsite maintenance or repairs.
3. Sanitizes the equipment to remove all information from associated media prior to removal from organizational facilities for offsite maintenance or repairs.
4. Checks all potentially impacted security controls to verify that the controls are still functioning properly following maintenance or repair actions.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - The organization maintains maintenance records for the system that include (a) the date and time of maintenance; (b) name of the individual performing the maintenance; (c) name of escort, if necessary; (d) a description of the maintenance performed; and (e) a list of equipment removed or replaced (including identification numbers, if applicable).
Requirement Enhancement 2 - The organization employs automated mechanisms to schedule and document maintenance and repairs as required, producing up-to-date, accurate, complete, and available records of all maintenance and repair actions, needed, in process, and completed.', N'The control is intended to address the security aspects of the organization’s system maintenance program. All maintenance activities to include routine, scheduled maintenance and repairs are controlled whether performed on site or remotely and whether the equipment is serviced on site or removed to another location. Maintenance procedures that require the physical removal of any control system component need to be documented, listing the date, time, reason for removal, estimated date of reinstallation, and name personnel removing components. These activities need to be approved by the appropriate organization officials. If the control system or component requires offsite repair, the organization removes all critical/sensitive information from associated media using approved procedures. After maintenance is performed on the control system, the organization checks the security features to ensure that they are still functioning properly.

', NULL, N'The organization:

1. Schedules, performs, documents, and reviews records of maintenance and repairs on system components in accordance with manufacturer or vendor specifications and/or organizational requirements
2. Explicitly approves the removal of the system or system components from organizational facilities for offsite maintenance or repairs
3. Sanitizes the equipment to remove all information from associated media prior to removal from organizational facilities for offsite maintenance or repairs
4. Checks all potentially impacted security controls to verify that the controls are still functioning properly following maintenance or repair actions.
', NULL, N'1. The organization maintains maintenance records for the system that include (a) the date and time of maintenance; (b) name of the individual performing the maintenance; (c) name of escort, if necessary; (d) a description of the maintenance performed; and (e) a list of equipment removed or replaced (including identification numbers, if applicable).
2. The organization employs automated mechanisms to schedule and document maintenance and repairs as required, producing up-to-date, accurate, complete, and available records of all maintenance and repair actions, needed, in process, and completed.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Periodic System Maintenance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security is most effective when it is designed into the control system at the earliest stages of development and sustained, using effective maintenance, throughout the life cycle of the system and through all future configurations. Periodic system maintenance is an aspect of system development and maintenance that addresses the security aspects of the system maintenance program.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Schedules, performs, documents, and reviews records of maintenance and repairs on system components in accordance with manufacturer or vendor specifications and/or organizational requirements</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Explicitly approves the removal of the system or system components from organizational facilities for offsite maintenance or repairs</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Sanitizes the equipment to remove all information from associated media prior to removal from organizational facilities for offsite maintenance or repairs</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">Checks all potentially impacted security controls to verify that the controls are still functioning properly following maintenance or repair actions.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The control is intended to address the security aspects of the organization’s system maintenance program. All maintenance activities to include routine, scheduled maintenance and repairs are controlled whether performed on site or remotely and whether the equipment is serviced on site or removed to another location. Maintenance procedures that require the physical removal of any control system component need to be documented, listing the date, time, reason for removal, estimated date of reinstallation, and name personnel removing components. These activities need to be approved by the appropriate organization officials. If the control system or component requires offsite repair, the organization removes all critical/sensitive information from associated media using approved procedures. After maintenance is performed on the control system, the organization checks the security features to ensure that they are still functioning properly.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization maintains maintenance records for the system that include (a) the date and time of maintenance; (b) name of the individual performing the maintenance; (c) name of escort, if necessary; (d) a description of the maintenance performed; and (e) a list of equipment removed or replaced (including identification numbers, if applicable).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs automated mechanisms to schedule and document maintenance and repairs as required, producing up-to-date, accurate, complete, and available records of all maintenance and repair actions, needed, in process, and completed.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 MA-2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.4.1</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.7', N'2.10.7', N'Maintenance Tools', N'Maint Tools', N'The organization approves and monitors the use of system maintenance tools.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - The organization inspects all maintenance tools carried into a facility by maintenance personnel for obvious improper modifications.
Requirement Enhancement 2 - The organization checks all media containing diagnostic and test programs for malicious code before the media are used in the system.
Requirement Enhancement 3 - The organization prevents the unauthorized removal of maintenance equipment by one of the following: (a) verifying that no organizational information is contained on the equipment, (b) sanitizing or destroying the equipment, (c) retaining the equipment within the facility, or (d) obtaining an exemption from a designated organization official explicitly authorizing removal of the equipment from the facility.
Requirement Enhancement 4 - The organization employs automated mechanisms to restrict the use of maintenance tools to authorized personnel only.
Requirement Enhancement 5 - Maintenance tools are used with care on control system networks to ensure that control system operations will not be degraded by their use.', N'The intent of this control is to address the security-related issues arising from the hardware and software brought into the system specifically for diagnostic and repair actions (e.g., a hardware or software packet sniffer that is introduced for the purpose of a particular maintenance activity). Hardware and software components that may support system maintenance, yet are a part of the system (e.g., the software implementing “ping,” “ls,” “ipconfig,” or the hardware and software implementing the monitoring port of an Ethernet switch), are not covered by this control.

', NULL, N'The organization approves and monitors the use of system maintenance tools.

', NULL, N'1. The organization inspects all maintenance tools carried into a facility by maintenance personnel for obvious improper modifications.
2. The organization checks all media containing diagnostic and test programs for malicious code before the media are used in the system.
3. The organization prevents the unauthorized removal of maintenance equipment by one of the following: (a) verifying that no organizational information is contained on the equipment, (b) sanitizing or destroying the equipment, (c) retaining the equipment within the facility, or (d) obtaining an exemption from a designated organization official explicitly authorizing removal of the equipment from the facility.
4. The organization employs automated mechanisms to restrict the use of maintenance tools to authorized personnel only.
5. Maintenance tools are used with care on control system networks to ensure that control system operations will not be degraded by their use.
', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Maintenance Tools</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security is most effective when it is designed into the control system at the earliest stages of development and sustained, using effective maintenance, throughout the life cycle of the system and through all future configurations. Maintenance Tools is an aspect of system development and maintenance that deals with control of the tools, including software, that are used for maintenance.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization approves and monitors the use of system maintenance tools.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The intent of this control is to address the security-related issues arising from the hardware and software brought into the system specifically for diagnostic and repair actions (e.g., a hardware or software packet sniffer that is introduced for the purpose of a particular maintenance activity). Hardware and software components that may support system maintenance, yet are a part of the system (e.g., the software implementing “ping,” “ls,” “ipconfig,” or the hardware and software implementing the monitoring port of an Ethernet switch), are not covered by this control.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization inspects all maintenance tools carried into a facility by maintenance personnel for obvious improper modifications.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization checks all media containing diagnostic and test programs for malicious code before the media are used in the system.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization prevents the unauthorized removal of maintenance equipment by one of the following: (a) verifying that no organizational information is contained on the equipment, (b) sanitizing or destroying the equipment, (c) retaining the equipment within the facility, or (d) obtaining an exemption from a designated organization official explicitly authorizing removal of the equipment from the facility.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization employs automated mechanisms to restrict the use of maintenance tools to authorized personnel only.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Maintenance tools are used with care on control system networks to ensure that control system operations will not be degraded by their use.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 MA-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 5.8, Annex B.4.11</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.4.2</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.8', N'2.10.8', N'Maintenance Personnel', N'Maint Personnel', N'The organization documents authorization and approval policies and procedures and maintains a list of personnel authorized to perform maintenance on the control system. Only authorized and qualified organization or vendor personnel perform maintenance on the control system.', N'Maintenance personnel need to have appropriate access authorization to the control system when maintenance activities allow access to organizational information that could result in a future compromise of availability, integrity, or confidentiality. When maintenance personnel do not have needed access authorizations, organizational personnel with appropriate access authorizations supervise maintenance personnel during the performance of maintenance activities on the control system.

', NULL, N'The organization documents authorization and approval policies and procedures and maintains a list of personnel authorized to perform maintenance on the control system. Only authorized and qualified organization or vendor personnel perform maintenance on the control system.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Maintenance Personnel</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Security is most effective when it is designed into the control system at the earliest stages of development and sustained, using effective maintenance, throughout the life cycle of the system and through all future configurations. Maintenance personnel is an aspect of system development and maintenance that deals with the authorization process for maintenance personnel.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization documents authorization and approval policies and procedures and maintains a list of personnel authorized to perform maintenance on the control system. Only authorized and qualified organization or vendor personnel perform maintenance on the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Maintenance personnel need to have appropriate access authorization to the control system when maintenance activities allow access to organizational information that could result in a future compromise of availability, integrity, or confidentiality. When maintenance personnel do not have needed access authorizations, organizational personnel with appropriate access authorizations supervise maintenance personnel during the performance of maintenance activities on the control system.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 MP-5</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.1, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. B.1.22, App. C.4.3</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.10.9', N'2.10.9', N'Non-Local (Remote) Maintenance', N'Remote Maint', N'The organization:

1. Authorizes and monitors and controls remotely executed maintenance and diagnostic activities.
2. Allows the use of remote maintenance and diagnostic tools only as consistent with organizational policy and documented in the security plan for the system.
3. Maintains records for remote maintenance and diagnostic activities.
4. Terminates all sessions and remote connections when remote maintenance is completed.
5. Changes passwords following each remote maintenance session if password-based authentication is used to accomplish remote maintenance.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - The organization audits remote maintenance and diagnostic sessions, and designated organizational personnel review the maintenance records of the remote sessions.
Requirement Enhancement 2 - The organization documents the installation and use of remote maintenance and diagnostic links.
Requirement Enhancement 3 - The organization: 
a. Requires that remote maintenance or diagnostic services be performed from a system that implements a level of security at least as high as that implemented on the system being serviced or 
b. Removes the component to be serviced from the system and prior to remote maintenance or diagnostic services, sanitizes the component (e.g., clearing of set points, embedded network addresses and embedded security validation information) before removal from organizational facilities. After the service is performed and the component is returned to the facility, the organization should check or reinstall the authorized firmware code as specified by the configuration management plan and reset all authorized embedded configuration settings. This should remove potentially malicious software that may have been added via "new" firmware. This should be done before reconnecting the component to the system.
Requirement Enhancement 4 - The organization requires that remote maintenance sessions be protected by a strong authenticator tightly bound to the user.
Requirement Enhancement 5 - The organization requires that 
a. Maintenance personnel notify the system administrator when remote maintenance is planned (i.e., date/time)
b. A designated organizational official with specific security/system knowledge approves the remote maintenance.
Requirement Enhancement 6 - The organization employs cryptographic mechanisms to protect the integrity and confidentiality of remote maintenance and diagnostic communications.
Requirement Enhancement 7 - The organization employs remote disconnect verification at the termination of remote maintenance and diagnostic sessions.', N'Individuals communicating through an external, nonorganization-controlled network (e.g., the Internet) conduct remote maintenance and diagnostic activities. Identification and authentication techniques used in remote maintenance and diagnostic sessions are consistent with Section 2.15.1, “Access Control Policy and Procedures.” Strong authentication and enforcement requirements are in other controls in Section 2.8, “System and Communications Protection”; Section 2.10, “System Development and Maintenance”; Section 2.14, “System and Information Integrity”; and Section 2.15, “Access Controls.”

