', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Best Practices</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Monitoring and reviewing the performance of an organization''s cyber and control system security policy provides the organization the ability to evaluate the performance and effectiveness of its security program. Best practices is an aspect of monitoring and reviewing control system security policy that deals with the incorporation of industry best practices.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization incorporates industry best practices into the organization’s security program for control systems.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Best practices include, but are not be limited to:</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="Decimal"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Industry events that identify failed and successful cybersecurity breaches</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Actions to be taken to resolve a breach of cybersecurity that are defined in light of the</Paragraph><List MarkerStyle="LowerLatin" Margin="Auto,0,Auto,0" Padding="Auto,0,Auto,0"><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Business priorities</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Processes employed to collect metrics (e.g., audits, incidents) that help verify that the cybersecurity activities (manual or automated) are performing as expected</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Process that will trigger a review of the level of residual risk and acceptable risk taking when changes exist to the organization, technology, business objectives, and processes</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">External events including identified threats and changes in social climate</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Operational data analyzed, recorded, and reported to assess the effectiveness or performance of the cybersecurity management system.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></ListItem></List></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 CA-7</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">20 Critical Security Controls Twenty Critical Controls for Effective Cyber Defense: Consensus Audit CC-17</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 1.2</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) CIPS CIP 002-3 through CIP 009-3</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 App. C.3.5</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.17.5', N'2.17.5', N'Security Accreditation', N'Sec Accreditation', N'The organization authorizes (i.e., accredits) the control system for processing before operations and periodically updates the authorization based on organization-defined frequency or when a significant change occurs to the system. A senior organizational official signs and approves the security accreditation.', N'The organization assesses the security mechanisms employed within the control systems before and in support of the security accreditation. Security assessments conducted in support of security accreditations are called security certifications and need to be reviewed annually.

', NULL, N'The organization authorizes (i.e., accredits) the control system for processing before operations and periodically updates the authorization based on organization-defined frequency or when a significant change occurs to the system. A senior organizational official signs and approves the security accreditation.

', NULL, N'', NULL, N'<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"><Paragraph Style="{DynamicResource ParagraphTitleFlowDocument}">Security Accreditation</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">Monitoring and reviewing the performance of an organization''s cyber and control system security policy provides the organization the ability to evaluate the performance and effectiveness of its security program. Security accreditation is an aspect of monitoring and reviewing control system security policy that deals with authorization of the control system for its intended purpose.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement</Paragraph><Section Style="{DynamicResource SectionBackgroundFlowDocument}"><Paragraph FontStyle="Italic" FontSize="12">The organization authorizes (i.e., accredits) the control system for processing before operations and periodically updates the authorization based on organization-defined frequency or when a significant change occurs to the system. A senior organizational official signs and approves the security accreditation.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Supplemental Guidance</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">The organization assesses the security mechanisms employed within the control systems before and in support of the security accreditation. Security assessments conducted in support of security accreditations are called security certifications and need to be reviewed annually.</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">Requirement Enhancements</Paragraph><Section><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">None</Paragraph></Section><Paragraph FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13">References</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 3 CA-6</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">American Petroleum Institute Pipeline SCADA Security Standard API 1164 2nd Edition 3.6, Annex A</Paragraph><Paragraph FontStyle="Normal" FontSize="12">NRC Cyber Security Programs for Nuclear Facilities Regulatory Guide 5.71 C.3.3</Paragraph></FlowDocument>')
', NULL, N'Administrative-2.17.6', N'2.17.6', N'Security Certification', N'Sec Certification', N'The organization assesses the security mechanisms in the control system to determine the extent the security measures are implemented correctly, operating as intended, and producing the desired outcome with respect to meeting the security requirements for the system.
Requirement Enhancement 1 - The organization employs an independent certification agent or certification team to assess the security mechanisms in the control system.
Requirement Enhancement 2 - An independent certification agent or certification team is any individual or group capable of conducting an impartial assessment of an organizational control system. Impartiality implies that the assessors are free from any perceived or actual conflicts of interest with respect to the developmental, operational, and/or management chain of command associated with the control system or to the determination of security control effectiveness. Independent security certification services can be obtained from other elements within the organization or can be contracted to a public or private sector entity outside the organization.
Requirement Enhancement 3 - Contracted certification services are considered independent if the control system owner is not directly involved in the contracting process or cannot unduly influence the independence of the certification agent or certification team conducting the assessment of the security mechanisms in the control system.
Requirement Enhancement 4 - The authorizing official decides on the required level of certifier independence based on the criticality and sensitivity of the control system and the ultimate risk to organizational operations and organizational assets and to individuals. The authorizing official determines if the level of certifier independence is sufficient to provide confidence that the assessment results produced are sound and can be used to make a credible, risk-based decision.
Requirement Enhancement 5 - In special situations, for example when the organization that owns the control system is small or the organizational structure requires that the assessment of the security mechanisms be accomplished by individuals that are in the developmental, operational, and/or management chain of the system owner or authorizing official, independence in the certification process can be achieved by ensuring the assessment results are carefully reviewed and analyzed by an independent team of experts to validate the completeness, consistency, and veracity of the results.
Requirement Enhancement 6 - The authorizing official should consult with representatives of the appropriate regulatory bodies, the senior agency information security officer, and the chief information officer to fully discuss the implications of any decisions on certifier independence in the types of special circumstances described above.', N'Assessments are performed and documented by qualified assessors as authorized by the organization. External audits are outside the scope of this requirement. Ensure that the assessments do not interfere with control system functions. Care must be taken to ensure that the assessments do not interfere with control system functions. The assessor fully understands the corporate cyber and control system security policies and procedures and the specific health, safety, and environmental risks associated with a particular facility and/or process. A control system may need to be taken offline, to the extent feasible, before the assessments can be conducted. If a control system must be taken offline for assessments, assessments are scheduled to occur during planned control system outages whenever possible.

', NULL, N'The organization assesses the security mechanisms in the control system to determine the extent the security measures are implemented correctly, operating as intended, and producing the desired outcome with respect to meeting the security requirements for the system.

', NULL, N'1. The organization employs an independent certification agent or certification team to assess the security mechanisms in the control system.
2. An independent certification agent or certification team is any individual or group capable of conducting an impartial assessment of an organizational control system. Impartiality implies that the assessors are free from any perceived or actual conflicts of interest with respect to the developmental, operational, and/or management chain of command associated with the control system or to the determination of security control effectiveness. Independent security certification services can be obtained from other elements within the organization or can be contracted to a public or private sector entity outside the organization.
3. Contracted certification services are considered independent if the control system owner is not directly involved in the contracting process or cannot unduly influence the independence of the certification agent or certification team conducting the assessment of the security mechanisms in the control system.
4. The authorizing official decides on the required level of certifier independence based on the criticality and sensitivity of the control system and the ultimate risk to organizational operations and organizational assets and to individuals. The authorizing official determines if the level of certifier independence is sufficient to provide confidence that the assessment results produced are sound and can be used to make a credible, risk-based decision.
5. In special situations, for example when the organization that owns the control system is small or the organizational structure requires that the assessment of the security mechanisms be accomplished by individuals that are in the developmental, operational, and/or management chain of the system owner or authorizing official, independence in the certification process can be achieved by ensuring the assessment results are carefully reviewed and analyzed by an independent team of experts to validate the completeness, consistency, and veracity of the results.
6. The authorizing official should consult with representatives of the appropriate regulatory bodies, the senior agency information security officer, and the chief information officer to fully discuss the implications of any decisions on certifier independence in the types of special circumstances described above.
