users; >100 access points)', N'Review the network diagrams for all existing wireless access points.', 1, N'Wireless network access', N'Wireless networks use radio waves to connect devices, such as mobile devices, laptops, desktops, and printers, to the Internet and to the financial institution''s network and systems. Wireless networks can affect the financial institution''s risk profile in a variety of ways, depending on how the technology is used. Because wireless network standards continue to emerge and evolve, potential users face challenging questions about how to obtain the necessary technical expertise and whether to be early adopters or wait for proven standards. Failure to keep abreast of changing standards can expose the financial institution to strategic and reputation risks. For the wireless access point to be considered separate, the access point should reside outside of the financial institution''s private network (it can be located in the DMZ) and have its own dedicated Internet connection. "Logically separated" means that the guest wireless access point uses the same Internet connection as the financial institution, but this connection is logically separate via a virtual local area network.', N'IRP')
<10% of employees (staff, executives, managers) and board; e-mail access only', N'Multiple device types used; available to
<25% of authorized employees (staff, executives, managers) and board; e-mail and some applications accessed', N'Any device type used; available to >25% of employees (staff, executives, managers) and board; all applications accessed', N'Review list of non-owned CU devices that access the network.', 1, N'Personal devices allowed to connect to the corporate network', N'Types of devices include personal computers, laptops, and mobile devices. Allowing employees to use personal mobile devices is typically referred to as "bring your own device" (BYOD), which refers to permitted use of personal devices, such as laptops, tablets, mobile phones, and other devices owned by employees and board members. Device types can be one type (e.g., a specific brand of mobile phone using a specific wireless carrier) or multiple device types (e.g., laptops, tablets, iPads, and mobile phones of varying brands and wireless carriers). The degree of access may be limited to e-mail or involve specific applications or all applications on the financial institution''s private network.', N'IRP')
access; high level of complexity in terms of how they access systems', N'Substantial number of third parties (>25) and substantial number of individuals from third parties (>1,500) with access; high complexity in how they access systems', N'Review the listing of third-party service providers and the number of their employees.  Discuss with management the complexity (security of the connection) with which third parties access credit union systems.', 1, N'Third parties, including number of organizations and number of individuals from vendors and subcontractors, with access to internal systems (e.g., virtual private network, modem, intranet, direct connection)', N'This statement covers the numbers of third parties and individuals who are employed directly or indirectly by those third parties with access to the financial institution''s private network. A third-party is an external entity or individual with an established business arrangement, by contract or otherwise, with the financial institution. The complexity of how third parties access systems deals with the type of connection that is used by the third-party and can consist of a virtual private network (VPN) to secure the connection, modem, other Internet-based connection, a connection from a device located at the financial institution, and other forms of connectivity.', N'IRP')
30)', N'Several applications (31–75)', N'Significant number of applications (76–200)', N'Substantial number of applications and complexity (>200)', N'Review the inventory of software and or applications.  Discuss with management which applications are internally hosted, vendor-developed, and supporting critical activities. ', 1, N'Internally hosted, vendor-developed applications supporting critical activities', N'Internally hosted applications are run in-house and managed by financial institution staff (e.g., a mainframe running the core system software). Third-party developed applications are typically purchased by the financial institution with minimal or no changes made to the software. The most common forms of vendor-supplied software supporting critical activities include core banking, trust, accounts receivable factoring, leasing, and mortgage origination systems. Critical activities are defined based on the financial institution''s risk assessment and typically consider impact to operations and services.', N'IRP')
technologies', N'501–2,500
technologies', N'>2,500 technologies', N'Review the inventory of internally developed software, applications, or technologies.   Discuss with management which applications are internally hosted, user-developed technologies ( non-professionals), and supporting critical activities. ', 1, N'User-developed technologies and user computing that support critical activities (includes Microsoft Excel spreadsheets and Access databases or other user-developed tools)', N'User-developed technologies are created by internal staff, who in turn use the corresponding technology to perform daily duties. Typically, this type of technology is developed by individuals who are not professional developers. The most common example of a user-developed technology is a spreadsheet or database with sensitive customer or financial information that in some instances can support a critical business function.', N'IRP')
