<li>The organization stores all service continuity plans for the critical service in a controlled manner, and the plans are available to some who need to know.</li>
<li>Or; the organization stores some service continuity plans for the critical service in a controlled manner, and those plans are available to all who need to know.</li>
</ul>
')
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 94, 23, NULL, NULL, 740, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are mechanisms (e.g., failsafe, load balancing, hot swap capabilities) implemented to achieve resilience requirements in normal and adverse situations?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>Mechanisms are implemented to achieve resilience requirements for normal and adverse situations, where appropriate, for all assets that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>Mechanisms are implemented to achieve resilience requirements for normal and adverse situations, where appropriate, for some assets.</li>
</ul>
')
<ul>
<li>Service continuity plans often overlap and rely on the same set of organizational resources. For example:</li>
<li>An offsite facility that is named in more than one plan may not be able to satisfy requirements if multiple plans are executed simultaneously.</li>
<li>Multiple service continuity plans, which may need to be executed simultaneously, often rely on the same people.</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 95, 23, NULL, NULL, 740, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are plans reviewed to identify and resolve conflicts?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>The organization reviews all service continuity plans that support the critical service to identify and resolve conflicts.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>The organization reviews some service continuity plans to identify and resolve conflicts.</li>
</ul>
')
 •     The objective of service continuity plan testing is to ensure that plans work as intended.
 •     Testing identifies required improvements to the service continuity plans as well as the associated test plans.

The evaluation of test results involves comparing the documented test results against the established test objectives:
 •     Areas where objectives could not be met are recorded, and strategies are developed to review and revise the plans.
 •     Improvements to the testing process and plans should also be identified, documented, and incorporated into future tests.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 96, 23, NULL, NULL, 740, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Service with test objectives to identify needed improvements to service continuity plans?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     Test results for each service continuity plan required for the delivery of the critical service are compared with test objectives to identify needed improvements.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     Test results for some service continuity plans are compared with test objectives to identify needed improvements.
')
 •     The organization must be able to determine when the plan must be executed and who is responsible for initiating action.
 •     The organization should ensure that owners of service continuity plans understand the conditions for execution.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 97, 23, NULL, NULL, 740, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Have conditions been identified that trigger the execution of the service continuity plan?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     Conditions that trigger the execution of each service continuity plan that supports the critical service have been identified and documented.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     Conditions that trigger the execution of some service continuity plans have been identified and documented.
')
 •     As a result of the review of executed service continuity plans, improvements are identified and documented.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 98, 23, NULL, NULL, 740, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are improvements identified as a result of executing service continuity plans?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     Improvements to each executed service continuity plan that supports the critical service are identified and documented.
 •     Or; if service continuity plans have not been executed, the identification of improvements to executed plans is required as part of a standard process.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     Improvements to some executed service continuity plans are identified and documented.
')
 •     Threat monitoring is a process of data collection and distribution with the purpose of providing timely, accurate, complete, and relevant information about the organization''s current threat environment.
 •     Threat monitoring is an integral part of establishing a common operating picture for the organization.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 99, 23, NULL, NULL, 741, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Has responsibility for monitoring sources of threat information been assigned?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>The responsibility for monitoring all sources of threat information relevant to the critical service has been assigned.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>The responsibility for monitoring some sources of threat information has been assigned.</li>
</ul>
')

Internal stakeholders are identified to:
 •     Ensure communications about ongoing threat monitoring activities
 •     Promote threat awareness
 •     Ensure that the organization is performing under a common operating picture.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 100, 23, NULL, NULL, 742, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Have internal stakeholders (such as the critical service owner and incident management staff) been identified to whom threat information must be communicated?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>All internal stakeholders who must receive threat information have been identified and documented.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>Some internal stakeholders have been identified and documented.</li>
</ul>
')

External stakeholders are identified to:
 •     ensure communications about ongoing threat monitoring activities
 •     promote threat awareness
 •     ensure that the organization and its external stakeholders are performing under a common operating picture

External stakeholders may have a stated role in communication plans or the service continuity plans of the organization.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 101, 23, NULL, NULL, 742, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Have external stakeholders (such as emergency management personnel, regulatory, and information sharing organizations) been identified to whom threat information must be communicated?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>All external stakeholders who must receive threat information have been identified and documented.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>Some external stakeholders have been identified and documented.</li>
</ul>
')
 •     Threat information must be communicated according to established requirements.
 •     Communication requirements may dictate that various communications methods and channels should be considered and identified.
 •     The infrastructure to support those methods may need to be developed and implemented.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 102, 23, NULL, NULL, 742, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Is threat information communicated to stakeholders?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>Threat information is communicated to all identified stakeholders as required.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>Threat information is communicated to some of the identified stakeholders.</li>
<li>Or; some threat information is communicated to all identified stakeholders.</li>
</ul>
')
 •     Resources must be available to meet threat communication requirements.
 •     The authority and accountability should be detailed in job descriptions.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 103, 23, NULL, NULL, 742, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Have resources been assigned authority and accountability for communicating threat information?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>The authority and accountability for communicating threat information has been assigned to all responsible resources.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>The authority and accountability for communicating threat information has been assigned to some of the responsible resources.</li>
</ul>
')
 •     Training must be provided to staff that support and enable communications procedures.
 •     A skills inventory and gap analysis may be used to identify training requirements.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 104, 23, NULL, NULL, 742, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Have resources been trained with respect to their specific role in communicating threat information?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>All resources assigned to perform threat communication activities have been trained.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>Some resources assigned to perform threat communication activities have been trained.</li>
</ul>
')
 •     The selection process and criteria should be designed to ensure that the selected entity can fully meet the organization''s established requirements.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 105, 23, NULL, NULL, 743, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Is the ability of external entities to meet resilience requirements of the critical service considered in the selection process?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     The ability to meet resilience requirements is considered when selecting all external entities that will support the critical service.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     The ability to meet resilience requirements is considered when selecting some external entities.
')
 •     Protection and sustainment requirements included in agreements will form the basis for monitoring the performance of the external entity.

Types of agreements may include:
 •     Contracts
 •     Service level agreements
 •     Memoranda of agreement
 •     Purchase orders
 •     Licensing agreements

The agreement should:
 •     Be enforceable by the organization
 •     Include detailed and complete requirements that must be met by the external entity
 •     Include required performance standards and/or work products
 •     Be updated to reflect changes in requirements over the life of the relationship
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 106, 23, NULL, NULL, 743, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are resilience requirements included in formal agreements with external entities?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     The organization includes resilience requirements in agreements with all external entities that support the critical service.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     The organization includes resilience requirements in agreements with some external entities.
')
 •     The range of corrective actions should be established in the agreement with the external entity.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 107, 23, NULL, NULL, 744, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are corrective actions taken as necessary to address issues with external entity performance (as related to resilience requirements)?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     The organization has taken corrective actions to address performance issues for all external entities that support the critical service.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     The organization has taken corrective actions to address performance issues for some external entities.
')
 •     Public services may be vital to a facility''s continued operation during a disruption.
 •     Thorough consideration of these services must be given for service continuity planning.
 •     Public services generally include services that are specific to the geographical region of the facility.

Public services include:
 •     Fire response and rescue services
 •     Local and federal law enforcement (police, National Guard, FBI, etc.)
 •     Emergency management services, including paramedics and first responders
 •     Other services, such as hazardous material control
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 108, 23, NULL, NULL, 745, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are public services on which the critical service depends (fire response and rescue services, law enforcement, etc.) identified?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     The organization has identified and documented all public services on which the critical service depends.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     Some public services are identified.
')
 •     Critical services may be dependent on infrastructure providers to remain viable.
 •     The organization must be prepared to address the loss of these providers, which can affect the resilience of the critical service.
 •     The organization may need to consider the resilience of the providers when developing service continuity plans.

These infrastructure services include:
 •     Telecommunications and telephone services
 •     Data and network service providers
 •     Electricity, natural gas, and other energy sources
 •     Water and sewer services
 •     Fuel providers for emergency power
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 109, 23, NULL, NULL, 745, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are infrastructure providers on which the critical service depends (telecommunications and telephone services, energy sources, etc.) identified?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     The organization has identified and documented all infrastructure providers on which the critical service depends.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     Some infrastructure providers are identified.
')

In the context of External Dependencies Management, identifying key assets is important because external entities may affect or otherwise have obligations with respect to these assets.

The following are examples of obligations or duties that external entities may have with respect to assets that support the critical service.
<ul>
<li>Suppliers may serve as the ‘containers'' or have other obligations with respect to information for which the organization is responsible. For example, an organization in the medical industry may entrust protected health information to a third party.</li>
<li>Suppliers may have other specific obligations relative to organization assets, for example, maintenance or other support.</li>
<li>Local or regional governmental authorities may have a role in sustaining or ensuring that key assets are available – for example, in securing or managing transportation networks used by the organization''s personnel.</li>
<li>External entities may be used as an entry point by malicious actors to harm or damage organization assets. For example, attackers may use access granted to a third party to enter and attack organization networks and systems.</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 110, 23, NULL, NULL, 746, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are the organization''s assets that directly support the critical service inventoried?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
<ul>
<li>The organization has documented in one or more repositories the people, information, technologies, and facilities essential to the operation of the critical service and this documentation is current.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
<ul>
<li>The organization has documented some assets that support the critical service, or it has documented some asset types but not others (for example, it documents the technology that supports the service but not the people), or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 111, 23, NULL, NULL, 747, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are the organization’s services identified and documented across the enterprise?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has documented all of its services as a part of an established business process and the current list of services is accurate.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The list of services is partially documented, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

A control objective is a performance target for a control or control system. Control objectives are important to external dependencies management because external entities are often responsible for establishing and maintaining controls over assets for which the organization is responsible. Control objectives provide a way to evaluate and correct the actions of third parties.
<ul>
<li>Control objectives provide a set of high-level requirements for the protection and sustainment of a critical service and associated assets.</li>
<li>Sources for identifying control objectives may be found in governance documents, policy documents, etc.</li>
</ul>

Examples of control objectives as they pertain to asset types: 
<ul>
<li>People: (1) prior to hiring candidates for employment, ensure they are trustworthy and reliable and (2) all outside support personnel are identified.</li>
<li>Information: (1) ensure the confidentiality and integrity of customers'' payment information and (2) information assets are disposed of according to policy.</li>
<li>Technology: (1) ensure the databases that support one or more critical services remain available and (2) network integrity is protected.</li>
<li>Facilities: (1) Ensure environmental systems are maintained at an appropriate level to support data center equipment and (2) physical access to assets is managed and protected.</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 112, 23, NULL, NULL, 747, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Have control objectives been established for organization assets that support the critical service(s)?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>Control objectives are established and documented for all assets that support the critical service (people, information, technology, and facilities).</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>Control objectives are established and documented for some assets that support the critical service, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Having an established process can help the organization make better selections, including choosing external entities that align more closely with the organization''s needs and requirements.

Having an established process means that:
<ul>
<li>There are specific steps or activities that organization staff must complete when completing the activity, in this case forming relationships on behalf of the organization.</li>
<li>The process is supported by documentation. The process need not be documented in one consolidated plan, but it should be documented to support the process. For example, staff responsible for sourcing suppliers may have written criteria for selecting suppliers. Staff may have a written checklist or other guidance to complete before submitting contracts for approval, etc.</li>
<li>The organization''s staff is aware that the process exists and that they are expected to follow it.</li>
<li>The process applies to formal agreements with any type of external entity to support the critical service.</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 113, 23, NULL, NULL, 748, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization have an established process for entering into formal agreements with external entities?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has an established process for entering into formal agreements with external entities that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>A process exists but some staff are not aware of it, or it is not supported by documentation, or it applies only to some types of external entities, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

It is not unusual for organizations to have a well-documented process for entering into formal agreements with suppliers, which does not take into consideration the resilience requirements of the critical service.

Some common reasons that the organization''s process to form external entity relationships may be deficient include:
<ul>
<li>The process may be dictated or determined by other organizational units and not designed with the critical service in mind.</li>
<li>The appropriate staff have not been included in the process.</li>
<li>The process may be exclusively focused on other objectives, for example achieving the lowest cost or preventing undue influence over the formation of contracts.</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 114, 23, NULL, NULL, 748, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization''s process to enter into formal agreements with suppliers ensure that resilience requirements are considered before entering into agreements?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization''s process to enter into formal agreements with external entities ensures resilience requirements are considered before entering into formal agreements.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>There are deficiencies in the process, for example, resilience requirements are only sometimes considered, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

A single point of failure means that a single disruption or interruption involving an external entity would cause a failure or significant disruption of the critical service. An example of a single point of failure is a supplier that is the sole source of a critical service or critical asset, and for whom there is no substitute readily available (i.e., on standby or actively operating in parallel).

This question asks about a specific risk management practice and requires that single points of failure be identified and managed as risks (i.e., formally accepted, mitigated, transferred, monitored, etc.).

Failure to recognize single points of failure may result in neither the organization nor relevant external entities putting sufficient controls and mitigations in place to guard against this risk.

Single points of failure need not be organizations with which the organization has a formal agreement or direct relationship. For example:
<ul>
<li>Two or more of the organization''s suppliers may rely on a third party or "second-tier" supplier.</li>
<li>Multiple suppliers (for example, two outsourced data centers) may be in the same geographic region and therefore subject to the same natural disaster.</li>
<li>Multiple suppliers may rely on the same infrastructure providers.</li>
</ul>
These ‘second-tier'' points of failure – which may affect multiple suppliers, for example – are also sometimes known as "common modes of failure."
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 115, 23, NULL, NULL, 749, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization identify and manage the risk of an external entity being a single point of failure?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization analyzes the external entities that support the critical service to identify single points of failure as a required part of risk management, and these risks are managed (impacts are identified and analyzed, and the disposition of risks assigned).</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization examines only some external entities that support the critical service for single points of failure, or risks are identified but not managed, or the practice appears to be otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

An operational risk management plan provides a common foundation for the performance of risk management activities. For External Dependencies Management, having a plan that includes external dependencies helps to coordinate management of these risks with the organization''s risk appetite and broader risk strategy.

Operational risk is defined as the potential impact on assets and their related services that could result from inadequate or failed internal processes, failures of systems or technology, deliberate or inadvertent actions of people, or external events (weather, disasters, war, etc.). Any of these sources of risk can affect both the organization directly and also the external entities on which the organization depends.

Typical items addressed in an operational risk management plan include:
<ul>
<li>The scope of operational risk management activities</li>
<li>The methods to be used for operational risk identification, analysis, mitigation, monitoring, and communication</li>
<li>The sources of operational risk</li>
<li>How the sources of operational risk should be organized, categorized, compared, and consolidated</li>
<li>Parameters for measuring and taking action on operational risks</li>
<li>Risk mitigation techniques to be used, such as the development of layered administrative, technical, and physical controls</li>
<li>Definition of risk measures to monitor the status of operational risks</li>
<li>Time intervals for risk monitoring and reassessment</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 116, 23, NULL, NULL, 749, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Has a plan for managing operational risk been established and agreed to by Stakeholders?', N'Criteria for "Implemented "response
<ul>
<li>There is a documented plan for managing operational risks, and this document is used to guide risk management.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The operational risk management plan is incomplete, or it exists but does not guide risk management in the organization, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

This practice is important because it provides prospective suppliers with notice of what will be required of them to support the critical service, including relevant roles and responsibilities for cybersecurity staff. It can also help reduce the likelihood that the organization will contract with a supplier that is incapable of meeting the critical service''s resilience requirements.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 117, 23, NULL, NULL, 750, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are resilience requirements included in written communications with prospective suppliers, for example in requests for proposals (RFPs)?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization includes applicable resilience requirements in communications with all prospective suppliers.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization includes applicable resilience requirements in communications with some prospective suppliers, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

A basic component of external dependencies management is to form relationships with external entities that are capable of supporting, protecting, and sustaining the critical service.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 118, 23, NULL, NULL, 750, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization consider the ability of suppliers to meet the resilience requirements of the critical service before entering into formal agreements?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The ability to meet resilience requirements is considered when selecting any supplier that supports the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The ability to meet resilience requirements is considered when selecting some suppliers, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

This practice helps the organization to have more confidence in the relationships it forms with external entities. It is also a frequent feature of compliance and regulatory guidelines. The organization can use a number of means to verify that external entities can meet specific resilience requirements, for example:
<ul>
<li>Third-party audit</li>
<li>Checklists</li>
<li>Examination of specific artifacts, for example, their incident response or business continuity plans</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 119, 23, NULL, NULL, 750, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization identify suppliers from which it requires documented verification of an ability to meet the critical service''s resilience requirements?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization identifies the need for documented verification of the supplier''s ability to meet resilience requirements, and obtains this verification every time it contemplates entering into a formal agreement with a supplier to support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization inconsistently requires verification that suppliers can meet resilience requirements, or the organization''s process is otherwise incomplete or ad hoc.</li>
</ul>
')

Including resilience requirements in formal agreements with suppliers that support critical services is an essential practice. Such requirements make it clear what suppliers are expected to do and the standards they are expected to meet. They also form the basis for other actions involving the formal agreement, for example, performance monitoring, detecting breach of the formal agreement, and termination if necessary.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 120, 23, NULL, NULL, 751, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are resilience requirements for the critical service included in formal agreements with suppliers?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" response
<ul>
<li>The organization includes resilience requirements in formal agreements with all suppliers that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization includes resilience requirements in agreements with some external entities, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Rarely do dependencies end with only the supplier with whom the organization is contracted. An external entity that fails to account for and manage its own external dependencies can represent an additional risk to the organization and critical service.

An affirmative answer to this question does not require that a formal agreement state verbatim "The supplier agrees to manage its own external dependencies." Formal agreement terms may refer more generally to a requirement that the resilience requirements of the critical service also apply to subcontractors, or impose a duty on the supplier to include these terms in its own contracts.

This practice is also sometimes referred to as including "flow-down" requirements in contracts.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 121, 23, NULL, NULL, 751, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Do formal agreements require suppliers to manage their own external dependencies?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization includes requirements in formal agreements with suppliers that the supplier will manage its own external dependencies to support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization includes requirements that some suppliers manage their own external dependencies, or the process to perform this practice is otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

This practice is important because it helps ensure that suppliers that support the critical service have adequate planning in place to protect and sustain the critical service.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 122, 23, NULL, NULL, 751, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Do formal agreements require that suppliers maintain disruption management plans (incident management, service continuity, etc.)?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has requirements or language in all agreements with suppliers that support the critical service to maintain service continuity or incident management plans, as appropriate, to protect and sustain the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has requirements or language in some agreements with relevant external entities to maintain disruption management plans, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

While vulnerabilities of suppliers represent potential risk to the organization, organization''s are typically constrained in their ability to manage vulnerabilities across arms-length agreements. The purpose of this practice is to ensure that suppliers accept responsibility to manage vulnerabilities as part of meeting the resilience requirements of the critical service.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 123, 23, NULL, NULL, 751, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Do formal agreements require that suppliers manage vulnerabilities that may affect the critical service?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>Formal agreements with all suppliers supporting the critical service include a requirement to manage vulnerabilities that affect the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>Formal agreements with some suppliers include requirements to manage vulnerabilities, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Without a clear understanding that suppliers will report incidents that affect the critical service, the organization may incur additional risk or be simply unaware that the critical service has been disrupted or otherwise compromised.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 124, 23, NULL, NULL, 751, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Do formal agreements with suppliers include requirements to report incidents that affect the critical service?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>All formal agreements with suppliers that support the critical service contain requirements for the suppliers to report incidents that may negatively affect the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>Some formal agreements with suppliers that support the critical service contain requirements for suppliers to report incidents that may negatively affect the critical service, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Writing and maintaining service continuity and incident management plans is particularly challenging when the critical service is supported by suppliers and other external entities. This is because the continuity of the critical service may depend on the actions of suppliers during a disruption, and because information that is necessary for planning purposes may reside with the external entity, rather than with the organization itself.

Examples of supplier participation in disruption planning and exercising may include:
<ul>
<li>Engagement in the organization''s incident management and service continuity plan development or maintenance processes</li>
<li>Accountability for the provision/maintenance of supplier information, such as contact(s), technology, or facility details</li>
<li>Provision of supplier incident management and continuity planning information</li>
<li>Required input to the organization''s incident and service</li>
<li>Continuity plan testing process</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 125, 23, NULL, NULL, 751, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Do formal agreements with suppliers that support the critical service require their participation in disruption management planning and exercising?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has language in all agreements with relevant suppliers requiring participation in the organization''s incident and service continuity planning and exercising activities.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has language in some agreements with relevant suppliers requiring participation in the organization''s incident management and service continuity planning activities, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

This practice is important because it helps the organization obtain technology that is more likely to satisfy the requirements and importance of the critical service.

An affirmative answer to this question requires that the organization has some process or have assigned responsibility to staff to become aware of vulnerabilities to technology that the organization intends to purchase to support the critical service. In practice, the organization''s ability to gather effective, complete vulnerability information may vary substantially depending on the specific technology item.

Some ways that the organization can evaluate technology vulnerabilities before acquisition include:
<ul>
<li>Incorporating vulnerability-reporting requirements in formal technology purchase agreements</li>
<li>Participating in ICS-CERT or other organizations to become aware of vulnerabilities</li>
<li>Reviewing system configurations for potential vulnerabilities</li>
<li>Communicating with peer organizations that also use particular external entities</li>
<li>Researching vulnerability information in publicly available databases and sources</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 126, 23, NULL, NULL, 752, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization evaluate technology assets that support the critical service for vulnerabilities before they are acquired?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization evaluates all technology assets to support the critical service for vulnerabilities before acquiring and deploying these assets internally.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization evaluates some technology assets for vulnerabilities before acquiring them, or this practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

In some cases acceptance testing can be expensive and beyond the capability of many organizations.

Therefore, an affirmative answer to this question does not require that testing is done in all cases. Instead, this practice asks if the organization has a decision making process to evaluate the need for this control, and whether it conducts acceptance testing if appropriate.

Acceptance testing involves verifying that acquired technology meets the organization''s resilience requirements for the critical service, for example confidentiality, integrity, availability, and maintainability, as well as other requirements that support the critical service.

For example, acceptance testing may be used to verify that
<ul>
<li>The technology has the necessary functionality or features, or</li>
<li>The technology is not counterfeit, or</li>
<li>The technology has not been maliciously tainted or tampered with</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 127, 23, NULL, NULL, 752, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization formally evaluate the need to conduct acceptance testing for technology assets that support the critical service and conduct such testing (if appropriate)?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has a process to identify acquired technology assets which should undergo acceptance testing prior to deployment, and it conducts the appropriate testing.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization conducts some acceptance testing of technology assets but does so inconsistently, or the organization has identified technology that should be subject to acceptance testing but does not conduct the testing, or the practice otherwise appears incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

This practice – to document requirements for technology that supports the critical service — is important because having documented requirements forms the basis for other activities, such as selecting trusted vendors and acceptance testing (if appropriate).

Having an established process means that:
<ul>
<li>There are specific steps or activities that organization staff must complete.</li>
<li>The process is supported by appropriate documentation. For example, there may be a documented schedule of meetings to identify requirements.</li>
<li>The organization''s staff is aware that the process exists and that they are expected to follow it.</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 128, 23, NULL, NULL, 752, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization identify and document the
resilience requirements for technology assets that support the critical service?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has a process to identify and document requirements for all technology that supports the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization''s process identifies and documents resilience requirements for some technology that supports the critical service, or the process appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

organizations can manage and reduce risks from integrity problems with hardware and software by only purchasing technology to support the critical service from trusted vendors. A basic practice is for organizations to have documented criteria for vendors to be considered trusted. These criteria often involve compliance with standards set forth by an acknowledged authority. Examples include:
<ul>
<li>The vendor has been selected as an authorized distributor by the OEM manufacturer.</li>
<li>The vendor may meet established standards set out by an industry organization (for example, The Open Group Trusted Technology Provider or similar standard).</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 129, 23, NULL, NULL, 752, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Has the organization identified the criteria or standards required for technology suppliers to be considered trusted?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has documented standards that describe when a technology provider is "trusted" for the purpose of providing technology to support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has partially documented criteria, or these criteria apply only to some types of technology, or this practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Using trusted suppliers for technology assets that support the critical service cannot provide complete assurance against counterfeits, malicious tampering, or similar threats. However, this is a basic control against this specific risk.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 130, 23, NULL, NULL, 752, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Has the organization identified trusted suppliers from which it obtains technology assets that support the critical service?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has a documented list of technology vendors who are designated as "trusted", from which it purchases technology that supports the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has a partially documented list of trusted suppliers, or the list exists but is used inconsistently, or this practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Managing vulnerabilities is a very important part of ensuring the resilience of critical services. Vulnerability management is complicated by supplier relationships, where the assets used to support the critical service are under the direct control of an external entity. The purpose of this practice is to ensure that suppliers who support the critical service are managing vulnerabilities that may affect the critical service.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 131, 23, NULL, NULL, 753, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization conduct periodic reviews with suppliers to verify that vulnerabilities relevant to the critical service are continuously managed?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization conducts reviews with suppliers according to a documented period to ensure that the relevant suppliers are managing vulnerabilities that may affect the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization conducts reviews with some suppliers, or the reviews are conducted inconsistently, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Periodically means that a specific period for the activity has been identified and documented by the organization.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 132, 23, NULL, NULL, 753, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization periodically review and update resilience requirements for suppliers?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization periodically reviews and updates the resilience requirements that apply to all suppliers.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization reviews and updates the resilience requirements for some suppliers, or the review is done inconsistently, or the practice otherwise appears incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Periodically means that a specific period for the activity has been identified and documented by the organization.

A key component of external dependencies management is active, ongoing risk management. This includes ongoing risk monitoring, assessments, and decision making.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 133, 23, NULL, NULL, 753, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization periodically review risks due to suppliers?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization periodically reviews risks due to all suppliers that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization reviews risks related to suppliers occasionally, but not according to any documented period or schedule, or risks due to some suppliers are reviewed, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

The critical service''s resilience requirements may not be codified in formal agreements with suppliers for a variety of reasons. For example, the requirements may have changed or the organization may have insufficient negotiating power to compel suppliers to agree to all of the relevant requirements.

For business reasons organizations will often accept this risk. However, the organization ultimately owns the risk of failures of the critical service. Therefore, any time a supplier agrees to support the critical service without sufficient protections and controls, this situation should be monitored as an open risk.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 134, 23, NULL, NULL, 753, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization''s risk monitoring include critical service resilience requirements not codified in supplier agreements?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization documents and monitors as risks all resilience requirements not codified in formal agreements.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization monitors some requirements not codified</li>
</ul>
')

Failures of suppliers to support the resilience requirements of the critical service have the potential to create risks and disruptions. For that reason the organization should document and monitor these performance problems as part of its ongoing risk monitoring.

Examples of supplier performance issues and concerns:
<ul>
<li>Incidents affecting the critical service</li>
<li>Process failures, for example failing to notify the organization of changes affecting the critical service</li>
<li>Non-responsiveness to inquiries</li>
<li>Non-available services</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 135, 23, NULL, NULL, 753, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization''s risk monitoring include supplier performance issues and concerns?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization includes performance problems and failures of suppliers to support resilience requirements as part of its documented, ongoing risk monitoring.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization includes some supplier performance problems as part of its risk monitoring, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 136, 23, NULL, NULL, 744, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are corrective actions evaluated to ensure issues are remedied?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization evaluates the corrective actions taken with all suppliers to ensure that issues are resolved.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization evaluates corrective actions taken with some suppliers, it evaluates corrective actions inconsistently, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Stakeholders should be advised of performance issues so that they can use this information to help manage external dependencies.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 137, 23, NULL, NULL, 744, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are issues with supplier performance documented and reported to appropriate stakeholders?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization documents and reports supplier performance issues to appropriate stakeholders.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization reports performance issues involving some suppliers to stakeholders, or it only reports issues to some stakeholders, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Suppliers often own or have direct control of the assets that support the critical service. In other cases, the organization may own the assets but a supplier may have defined obligations affecting the assets (for example, maintenance agreements or other support obligations).

Therefore, it is very important that the organization and relevant suppliers jointly coordinate changes to assets that support the critical service, as needed.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 138, 23, NULL, NULL, 754, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are changes to assets that support the critical service (whether located at the organization or at suppliers) coordinated between the organization and suppliers?
2.1  Information
2.2  Technology
2.3  Facilities
2.4  People', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>For each relevant supplier, a process is implemented that ensures the organization and supplier coordinate changes to assets that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>For some suppliers a process is implemented to coordinate changes, or the coordination of changes is inconsistent, or the practice is otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

When external entities support the critical service, the capacity of assets owned or managed by suppliers can affect the availability of the critical service. Therefore the organization should clearly define how it and the relevant suppliers will cooperatively manage the capacity of the critical service and supporting assets.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 139, 23, NULL, NULL, 750, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization manage the capacity of services and assets cooperatively with suppliers?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>For each supplier that supports the critical service, a process is implemented where the organization and the external entity cooperatively manage the capacity of the critical service and key assets.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>For some suppliers a process is implemented where capacity is jointly managed, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Some examples of changes that may affect the critical service include:
<ul>
<li>Changes to supplier financial stability</li>
<li>Changes in political stability in regions where facilities are located</li>
<li>Changes to staffing which may affect the quality of services</li>
<li>Changes in the composition of the supplier''s subcontractors or ‘second-tier'' suppliers</li>
<li>Impending regulatory or compliance requirements that may affect the supplier''s support to the organization</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 140, 23, NULL, NULL, 754, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization monitor for organizational changes at external entities - for example buy-outs, financial problems, political or civil problems - that may affect the critical service?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization monitors all suppliers for organizational changes that may impact the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization monitors some suppliers for changes, or it monitors inconsistently, or the process otherwise appears incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Learning from supplier transitions can help the organization to improve its external dependencies management processes and avoid similar problems in the future.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 141, 23, NULL, NULL, 755, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization use lessons learned from supplier transitions to refine its external dependencies management processes?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has a process so that lessons learned from supplier transitions are communicated to stakeholders to refine external dependencies management.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization communicates lessons learned to some stakeholders, or it uses lessons learned from supplier transitions inconsistently, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Transition - or exit - planning is an important part of managing dependencies on suppliers. Some examples of criteria that may cause the organization to terminate agreements include:
<ul>
<li>Poor performance</li>
<li>Strategic changes in the service supported</li>
<li>Cost</li>
<li>In-sourcing</li>
</ul>

Note: formal agreements or contracts typically contain clauses that describe the right to terminate the agreement. This question asks about a separate management practice: to identify what would trigger the organization to exercise its rights under the relevant contracts. By itself, the existence of a formal agreement does not satisfy this practice.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 142, 23, NULL, NULL, 755, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Has the organization identified criteria or conditions that would cause it to terminate supplier formal agreements?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has identified criteria that would cause it to terminate formal agreements with each of the relevant suppliers that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has identified criteria that would cause it to terminate formal agreements with some suppliers, or the practice is otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

These terminations may be driven by the organization, by the supplier, or by circumstances out of either party''s control.

For key suppliers which provide unique and essential services, the decision to transition to another sourcing strategy may require long lead times and careful planning to manage the risk. Lacking a plan for how to manage this transition could lead to significant impacts to the critical service(s).

Examples of planning measures and mitigations:
<ul>
<li>Maintaining contingency contracts with alternate suppliers</li>
<li>Maintaining an in-house capability</li>
<li>Identifying alternate suppliers</li>
<li>Planning communications and customer strategies to facilitate transition</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 143, 23, NULL, NULL, 755, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Has the organization planned the actions it will take to sustain the critical service if one or more supplier formal agreements are terminated (by either the organization or supplier)?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has planned and documented how it will manage supplier transitions in the event that formal agreements with suppliers are terminated.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has partially completed transition planning, or the planning only applies to some suppliers, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

When performance problems or other issues are identified at infrastructure and governmental services providers, key stakeholders in the organization should be alerted so that they can take required actions to manage any resulting risks.

Specific stakeholders may range from operations personnel, including, for example, managers who may need to be informed about preparedness exercises or infrastructure interruptions, to executives who may need to interface with government leaders to resolve issues. Stakeholders should understand their roles and responsibilities in external dependencies management activities.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 144, 23, NULL, NULL, 749, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are performance (or other) issues involving infrastructure providers and governmental services communicated to stakeholders for use in managing the dependency?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization escalates issues involving infrastructure providers and governmental services to relevant internal stakeholders to manage any resulting risks.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization escalates issues involving some infrastructure providers or governmental service providers, or it elevates issues to some stakeholders, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Monitoring of access activity can alert the organization to potential attempts to inappropriately access information, conduct probing or reconnaissance activity in organization networks, or otherwise disrupt the critical service.

This question asks about inappropriate access attempts made on the organization''s information, technology, and facilities that support the critical service.

Techniques to identify inappropriate access range from log analysis to other solutions, for example SIEM (Security Incident and Event Management) systems.

Depending on the technique used, activities to identify inappropriate access should be periodic, for example log analysis should be conducted based on a documented time interval to be a complete practice.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 145, 23, NULL, NULL, 744, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization identify inappropriate access attempts (for example by periodically reviewing access logs) by external entity personnel or systems to organization assets?
4.1  Information
4.2  Technology
4.3  Facilities', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has a process to identify inappropriate access attempts by external entities to the organization''s assets, in each of the three asset types.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization identifies inappropriate access attempts originating at some external entities, it conducts this activity to protect some assets in each category, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Because it is very common for assets that support the critical service to be provided, maintained, or housed by external entities, it is important that relevant external entities be aware of the organization''s criteria for incidents that affect the critical service. Organizations that do not establish and communicate these criteria can experience delays to become aware of – and respond to – incidents that affect the critical service.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 146, 23, NULL, NULL, 749, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Have incident declaration criteria that support the critical service been established and communicated to relevant external entities?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has established incident declaration criteria and communicated these to all of the relevant external entities that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has established incident declaration criteria and communicated them to some external entities, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

In enterprises where critical services depend on the performance and resilience of external entities, it is essential that disruption plans are periodically tested with the cooperation and participation of those external entities.

It is important to conduct exercises in a manner that simulates real events, during which various components and organizations may need to communicate, work together, and understand their roles and responsibilities to resolve an incident.

Note, this practice does not require that testing or exercises be done with all external entities at the same time.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 147, 23, NULL, NULL, 749, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are disruption management plans tested cooperatively with relevant suppliers?
1.1  Incident management
1.2  Service continuity', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization exercises/tests disruption management plans with relevant suppliers that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization tests disruption management plans with some suppliers, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Internal stakeholders should be advised of threats to external entities that are relevant to the organization''s critical service. Internal stakeholders may use this information for a variety of purposes:
<ul>
<li>To consider external entities'' susceptibility to threats when entering into new agreements</li>
<li>To change external entity selection criteria</li>
<li>To refine or adjust incident or intrusion detection systems to detect attacks involving external entities</li>
<li>To evaluate the external entity''s reactions and risk mitigation as part of performance management</li>
<li>To consider the existence of the threat when making risk management decisions</li>
</ul>

Threat information communications must be delivered on an as-needed basis, according to established requirements. Because threat information communications can be diverse, a broad array of procedures, practices, technology, and infrastructure to support those requirements may need to be developed and implemented. The infrastructure to support various communication methods (physical and technical) and channels must be developed and implemented. Through these actions, timely, relevant, consistent, high- quality, and purposeful communications can be delivered proactively or during an event, incident, or crisis.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 148, 23, NULL, NULL, 756, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are threats to external entities reported to internal stakeholders for use in managing the dependency?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization reports to internal stakeholders all threats to relevant external entities that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization reports threats to some internal stakeholders, or it reports threat information inconsistently, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Example activities include:
<ul>
<li>Threat or situational awareness reporting jointly developed or distributed to the organization and external entity stakeholders</li>
<li>Shared information repository or system where the organization and external entity can share information on threats</li>
<li>Threat monitoring and response teams comprising representatives from the organization and the external entity</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 149, 23, NULL, NULL, 756, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Do the organization and relevant external entities exchange information about threats to the critical service?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization exchanges threat information with all relevant external entities that support the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization exchanges threat information with some relevant external entities that support the critical service, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

organizations may choose to include threats to external entities as part of their threat monitoring activities in cases where, for example, a supplier lacks its own threat monitoring program. An organization may decide that sustaining the critical service requires a more complete understanding of threats to critical infrastructure than it receives through its relationships with the specific infrastructure providers.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 150, 23, NULL, NULL, 756, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization identify external entities that it should include as part of its threat monitoring activities?
3.1     Suppliers
3.2      Infrastructure providers', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has analyzed its external dependencies to identify external entities that it should include as part of its threat monitoring, and it monitors threats against these entities.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization has identified some external entities it should include as part of its threat monitoring, or it monitors threats to external entities inconsistently, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')

Example activities and groups include:
<ul>
<li>Industry or sector incident/cyber exercises</li>
<li>InfraGard</li>
<li>Sector Coordinating Councils</li>
<li>ISACs</li>
<li>Participation in industry groups such as the Council of Supply Chain Management</li>
</ul>
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 151, 23, NULL, NULL, 756, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Does the organization participate in or take advantage of industry consortia (i.e., InfraGard, Coordinating Councils, Council of Supply Chain Management) to detect threats to the organization and external entities?', N'Criteria for "Implemented" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization participates in activities or groups to help identify relevant threats to the critical service, and the information learned influences activities to protect and sustain the critical service.</li>
</ul>

Criteria for "In Progress" Response
<ul>
<li>The organization participates occasionally or inconsistently in groups to detect and identify threats to the organization or its suppliers, or it participates but the information learned does not discernibly influence activities to protect the critical service, or the practice appears otherwise incomplete.</li>
</ul>
')
 •     In order to determine what skills the organization must possess to meet its cyber security needs, baseline competencies must be established for the critical service.
 •     Baseline competencies represent the staffing and skill set needs, not what it currently has in terms of staff and skills.
 •     By determining the required skills, the appropriate target for a sufficient level of staffing and skills is established.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 152, 23, NULL, NULL, 757, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Have required cyber security skills been identified for specific roles (administrators, technicians, etc.) for the critical service?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     The organization has identified all required skills for the specific roles needed to support the critical service.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     The organization has identified some required skills for the specific roles.
')
 •     Awareness activities must meet the broad needs of staff members.
 •     The activities must be scheduled, advertised (if necessary), resourced, and tracked.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 153, 23, NULL, NULL, 758, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Are cyber security training activities for the critical service conducted?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     The organization has conducted cyber security awareness activities for all personnel assigned to support the critical service.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     The organization has conducted cyber security awareness activities for some personnel.
')
 •     A process should exist to determine the effectiveness of the awareness and training programs in meeting the needs of staff that support the critical service.
</div>', NULL, NULL, 1, 154, 23, NULL, NULL, 758, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, N'Is the effectiveness of the awareness and training programs evaluated?', N'<div class="sub-header-2">Implemented</div>
 •     The organization revises, as needed, all of the cyber security awareness and training activities that are in support of the critical service.

<div class="sub-header-2">In Progress</div>
 •     The organization revises, as needed, some of the cyber security awareness and training activities.
')

Capabilities for implementing the awareness and training plan must be established and maintained, including:
 •     The selection of appropriate training approaches
 •     Sourcing or developing training materials
 •     Obtaining appropriate instructors
